var tafsirData_27 = {
  "1": "\"Ṭâ-Sĩn. These are the verses of the Quran; the clear Book\" opens the chapter of the ant, and the verse is the teaching on the opening, and the teaching is the verse's content. The verse states the two parts: \"Ṭâ-Sĩn. These are the verses of the Quran; the clear Book\", and the word \"clear\" is 'mubin,' and the verse joins the letters and the description, and the commentators explain the meaning: the disconnected letters are the chapter's opening signature, and the clear Book is the verses' name, and the two are the verse's first stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the opening, and the teaching is that the letters of the language are the Book's letters, and the clarity is the Book's own witness, and the verse then closes, and the closing is the verse's second stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's design, and the design is the next verse's subject, and the subject is the guide, and the guide is the chapter's next teaching. The verse is the chapter's word on the opening, and the word is the verse's last stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the Book, and the teaching is that the clear Book is the guide and the good news, and the two are the chapter's next verses, and the next verses open the chapter's description of its own receivers, and the description is the chapter's word. The opening is the chapter's picture of the Book's standing, and the standing is the teaching, and the teaching is that the letters and the clarity are the one announcement, and the announcement is the chapter's word, and the word carries the chapter to its description of the believers, and the description is the chapter's next teaching. The letters and the clear Book are the one announcement, and the announcement teaches that the Book's clarity is its own witness, and the witness opens the chapter's description of its receivers.",
  "2": "\"˹It is˺ a guide and good news for the believers:\" continues the description, and the verse is the teaching on the guide, and the teaching is the verse's content. The verse continues: \"˹It is˺ a guide and good news for the believers:\" and the word \"guide\" is 'huda,' and the word \"good news\" is 'bushra,' and the verse names the Book's two faces, and the commentators explain the meaning: the guide is the road, and the good news is the reward, and the two are the verse's first stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the guide, and the teaching is that the Book leads and promises, and the verse then leaves the sentence to the next verse, and the leaving is the verse's second stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's design, and the design is the next verse's subject, and the subject is the believers, and the believers are the chapter's next teaching. The verse is the chapter's word on the guide, and the word is the verse's last stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the receivers, and the teaching is that the Book's two faces are the believers', and the believers' is the chapter's word. The two names are the chapter's picture of the Book's work, and the work is the teaching, and the teaching is that the guided and the promised are the one company, and the company is the chapter's word, and the word carries the description to its detail, and the detail is the chapter's next teaching. The two faces of the guide and the good news are the Book's work, and the work teaches that the guided and the promised are the one company, and the company is the believers'. The two names of the guide and the good news are the Book's own description, and the description teaches that the led and the promised are the one company of the believers.",
  "3": "\"˹those˺ who establish prayer, pay alms-tax, and have sure faith in the Hereafter\" completes the description, and the verse is the teaching on the believers, and the teaching is the verse's content. The verse completes the description: \"˹those˺ who establish prayer, pay alms-tax, and have sure faith in the Hereafter\", and the word \"establish\" is 'yuqimun,' and the verse names the three marks, and the commentators explain the meaning: the prayer and the alms and the certainty are the believers' three, and the three are the verse's first stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the believers, and the teaching is that the Book's receivers are known by the three, and the verse then closes, and the closing is the verse's second stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's design, and the design is the next verse's subject, and the subject is the deniers, and the deniers are the chapter's next teaching. The verse is the chapter's word on the believers, and the word is the verse's last stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the certainty, and the teaching is that the sure faith in the Hereafter is the three's ground, and the ground is the chapter's word. The three marks are the chapter's picture of the Book's receivers, and the receivers are the teaching, and the teaching is that the prayer and the alms and the certainty are the Book's fruit, and the fruit is the chapter's word, and the word carries the chapter to its contrast, and the contrast is the chapter's next teaching. The three marks of the prayer and the alms and the certainty are the Book's fruit, and the fruit teaches that the sure faith in the Hereafter is the three's ground. The three marks are the believers' description, and the description teaches that the sure faith in the Hereafter is the three's ground, for the certainty carries the prayer and the alms.",
  "4": "\"As for those who do not believe in the Hereafter, We have certainly made their ˹evil˺ deeds appealing to them, so they wander blindly\" states the contrast, and the verse is the teaching on the appealing, and the teaching is the verse's content. The verse states the contrast: \"As for those who do not believe in the Hereafter, We have certainly made their ˹evil˺ deeds appealing to them, so they wander blindly\", and the word \"appealing\" is 'zayyanna,' and the verse records the making-beautiful, and the commentators explain the meaning: the denier's own deeds are made beautiful to him as the earning of his denial, and the making is the verse's first stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the appealing, and the teaching is that the denied Hereafter makes the evil deed the loved, and the verse then states the result: \"so they wander blindly\", and the result is the verse's second stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the wandering, and the teaching is that the one whose evil is beautified is the one who wanders, and the verse then closes, and the closing is the verse's last stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's design, and the design is the next verse's subject, and the subject is the torment, and the torment is the chapter's next teaching. The verse is the chapter's word on the appealing, and the word is the chapter's teaching on the justice, and the teaching is that the beautified evil is the denier's own earning, and the earning is the chapter's word, and the word carries the contrast to its verdict, and the verdict is the chapter's next teaching. The making-beautiful of the evil is the verse's justice, and the justice teaches that the beautified evil is the denier's own earning, for the denied Hereafter makes the evil deed the loved.",
  "5": "\"It is they who will suffer a dreadful torment, and in the Hereafter they will ˹truly˺ be the greatest losers\" states the verdict, and the verse is the teaching on the torment, and the teaching is the verse's content. The verse states the verdict: \"It is they who will suffer a dreadful torment, and in the Hereafter they will ˹truly˺ be the greatest losers\", and the word \"dreadful\" is 'su',' and the word \"losers\" is 'akhsarun,' and the verse names the two ends, and the commentators explain the meaning: the dreadful is this world's, and the greatest loss is the next's, and the two are the verse's first stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the torment, and the teaching is that the denier's two ends are the one loss, and the verse then closes, and the closing is the verse's second stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's design, and the design is the next verse's subject, and the subject is the receiving, and the receiving is the chapter's next teaching. The verse is the chapter's word on the torment, and the word is the verse's last stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the measure, and the teaching is that the beautified evil and the greatest loss are the one road's two ends, and the road is the chapter's word. The verdict is the chapter's picture of the contrast's close, and the close is the teaching, and the teaching is that the two receivers and the two ends are the chapter's opening, and the opening is the chapter's word, and the word carries the chapter to its next word, and the next is the chapter's next teaching. The two ends of the dreadful and the greatest loss are the one road, and the road teaches that the beautified evil and the greatest loss are the denier's two ends.",
  "6": "\"And indeed, you ˹O Prophet˺ are receiving the Quran from the One ˹Who is˺ All-Wise, All-Knowing\" states the source, and the verse is the teaching on the receiving, and the teaching is the verse's content. The verse states the source: \"And indeed, you ˹O Prophet˺ are receiving the Quran from the One ˹Who is˺ All-Wise, All-Knowing\", and the word \"receiving\" is 'latulaqqa,' and the verse records the receiving, and the commentators explain the meaning: the Prophet's receiving is from the Wise and the Knowing, and the two names are the verse's first stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the receiving, and the teaching is that the Book's source is the two names, and the verse then closes, and the closing is the verse's second stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's design, and the design is the next verse's subject, and the subject is the fire, and the fire is the chapter's next teaching. The verse is the chapter's word on the receiving, and the word is the verse's last stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the source, and the teaching is that the received Book is the Wise's, and the Wise's is the chapter's word. The two names are the chapter's picture of the source's standing, and the standing is the teaching, and the teaching is that the wisdom and the knowledge are the Book's two guardians, and the guardians are the chapter's word, and the word opens the chapter's stories, and the stories are the chapter's next teaching, and the first story is Moses's, and Moses's is the chapter's next verses. The two names of the wisdom and the knowledge are the Book's guardians, and the guardians teach that the received Book is the Wise's, and the Wise's is the source's standing.",
  "7": "\"˹Remember˺ when Moses said to his family,\"\"I have spotted a fire. I will either bring you some directions from there, or a burning torch so you may warm yourselves\" opens the story of Moses, and the verse is the teaching on the fire, and the teaching is the verse's content. The verse quotes the word: \"˹Remember˺ when Moses said to his family, \"I have spotted a fire. I will either bring you some directions from there, or a burning torch so you may warm yourselves,\"\" and the word \"fire\" is 'naran,' and the verse records the spotting, and the commentators explain the meaning: Moses saw the fire on the journey and promised the family the directions or the torch, and the promising is the verse's first stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the fire, and the teaching is that the sought fire of the warmth was the found light of the prophecy, and the verse then closes, and the closing is the verse's second stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's design, and the design is the next verse's subject, and the subject is the calling, and the calling is the chapter's next teaching. The verse is the chapter's word on the fire, and the word is the verse's last stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the mercy, and the teaching is that the seeker of the small was given the great, and the giving is the chapter's word. The fire is the chapter's picture of the story's opening, and the opening is the teaching, and the teaching is that the bush's fire was the Lord's call, and the call is the chapter's word, and the word carries the story to its moment, and the moment is the chapter's next teaching. The fire of the journey is the story's opening, and the opening teaches that the seeker of the small was given the great, for the sought torch was the found light of the prophecy.",
  "8": "\"But when he came to it, he was called ˹by Allah˺,\"\"Blessed is the one at the fire, and whoever is around it! Glory be to Allah, the Lord of all worlds records the calling, and the verse is the teaching on the blessing, and the teaching is the verse's content. The verse quotes the calling: \"But when he came to it, he was called ˹by Allah˺, \"Blessed is the one at the fire, and whoever is around it! Glory be to Allah, the Lord of all worlds,\"\" and the word \"Blessed\" is 'burika,' and the verse records the first words of the calling, and the commentators explain the meaning: the blessing and the glorification are the calling's opening, and the opening is the verse's first stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the blessing, and the teaching is that the fire's ground was the blessed, and the verse then leaves the sentence to the next verse, and the leaving is the verse's second stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's design, and the design is the next verse's subject, and the subject is the identity, and the identity is the chapter's next teaching. The verse is the chapter's word on the blessing, and the word is the verse's last stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the scene, and the teaching is that the blessing of the place and the glory of the Lord are the call's two words, and the two are the chapter's word. The calling is the chapter's picture of the story's heart, and the heart is the teaching, and the teaching is that the fire of the bush carried the Lord's voice, and the voice is the chapter's word, and the word carries the story to its next stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's next teaching.",
  "9": "\"O Moses! It is truly I. I am Allah—the Almighty, All-Wise\" completes the calling, and the verse is the teaching on the identity, and the teaching is the verse's content. The verse completes the calling: \"O Moses! It is truly I. I am Allah—the Almighty, All-Wise\", and the word \"truly\" is 'innahu,' and the verse records the self-announcement, and the commentators explain the meaning: the calling named itself, and the naming is the verse's first stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the identity, and the teaching is that the fire's voice is the Lord's, and the verse then states the two names: \"the Almighty, All-Wise\", and the two names are the verse's second stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the two names, and the teaching is that the might and the wisdom are the calling's seal, and the verse then closes, and the closing is the verse's last stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's design, and the design is the next verse's subject, and the subject is the staff, and the staff is the chapter's next teaching. The verse is the chapter's word on the identity, and the word is the chapter's teaching on the moment, and the teaching is that the announcement of the Lord to the shepherd is the mission's beginning, and the beginning is the chapter's word, and the word carries the story to its signs, and the signs are the chapter's next verses, and the next verses tell of the staff and the hand, and the telling is the chapter's word. The self-announcement of the Lord is the moment's heart, and the heart teaches that the fire's voice is the Lord's, for the naming of the two names seals the calling. The self-announcement is the moment's heart, and the heart teaches that the fire's voice is the Lord's, for the naming of the two names seals the calling of the shepherd.",
  "10": "\"Now, throw down your staff!\"\" But when he saw it slithering like a snake, he ran away without looking back. ˹Allah reassured him,˺ \"O Moses! Do not be afraid! Messengers should have no fear in My presence continues the story, and the verse is the teaching on the staff, and the teaching is the verse's content. The verse records the command and the scene: \"Now, throw down your staff!\" But when he saw it slithering like a snake, he ran away without looking back. ˹Allah reassured him,˺ \"O Moses! Do not be afraid! Messengers should have no fear in My presence\", and the word \"slithering\" is 'tahtazzu,' and the verse records the staff's turning and the running, and the commentators explain the meaning: the first sign was the staff's snake, and the running was the human fear, and the reassurance is the verse's first stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the staff, and the teaching is that the sign's wonder is the sign's terror to the human, and the verse then states the reassurance: \"Messengers should have no fear in My presence\", and the reassurance is the verse's second stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the presence, and the teaching is that the presence of the Lord is the fear's end, and the verse then closes, and the closing is the verse's last stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's design, and the design is the next verse's subject, and the subject is the exception, and the exception is the chapter's next teaching. The verse is the chapter's word on the staff, and the word is the chapter's teaching on the fear, and the teaching is that the messenger's fear is met and the presence is the calm, and the calm is the chapter's word, and the word carries the story to its next stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's next teaching.",
  "11": "\"˹Fear is˺ only for those who do wrong. But if they later mend ˹their˺ evil ˹ways˺ with good, then I am certainly All-Forgiving, Most Merciful\" states the exception, and the verse is the teaching on the fear's boundary, and the teaching is the verse's content. The verse states the exception: \"˹Fear is˺ only for those who do wrong. But if they later mend ˹their˺ evil ˹ways˺ with good, then I am certainly All-Forgiving, Most Merciful\", and the word \"fear\" is 'khawf,' and the verse records the two halves, and the commentators explain the meaning: the fear in the presence is the wrongdoer's, and the mended wrong is the forgiven, and the two are the verse's first stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the fear's boundary, and the teaching is that the messenger's presence is the safety, and the wrongdoer's is the fear, and the verse then states the door: \"then I am certainly All-Forgiving, Most Merciful\", and the door is the verse's second stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the mercy, and the teaching is that the mending of the evil with the good is the door's key, and the verse then closes, and the closing is the verse's last stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's design, and the design is the next verse's subject, and the subject is the hand, and the hand is the chapter's next teaching. The verse is the chapter's word on the boundary, and the word is the chapter's teaching on the balance, and the teaching is that the fear and the forgiveness are the one presence's two faces, and the faces are the chapter's word, and the word carries the story to its second sign, and the sign is the chapter's next teaching. The two halves of the fear and the forgiveness are the one presence, and the presence teaches that the messenger's standing is the safety, and the wrongdoer's is the fear.",
  "12": "\"Now put your hand through ˹the opening of˺ your collar, it will come out ˹shining˺ white, unblemished. ˹These are two˺ of nine signs for Pharaoh and his people. They have truly been a rebellious people\"\" records the second sign, and the verse is the teaching on the hand, and the teaching is the verse's content. The verse records the command: \"Now put your hand through ˹the opening of˺ your collar, it will come out ˹shining˺ white, unblemished\", and the word \"shining\" is 'bayda',' and the verse records the hand's sign, and the commentators explain the meaning: the two signs of the staff and the hand are the nine's first two, and the two are the verse's first stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the hand, and the teaching is that the hand's shining is the second sign, and the verse then states the count: \"˹These are two˺ of nine signs for Pharaoh and his people\", and the count is the verse's second stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the signs, and the teaching is that the nine signs are the mission's equipment, and the verse then states the verdict: \"They have truly been a rebellious people\", and the verdict is the verse's last stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the audience, and the teaching is that the signs were sent to the rebellious, and the verse then closes, and the closing is the chapter's design, and the design is the next verse's subject, and the subject is the denial, and the denial is the chapter's next teaching. The verse is the chapter's word on the hand, and the word is the chapter's teaching on the equipment, and the teaching is that the nine signs are the known, and the known is the chapter's word, and the word carries the story to its next scene, and the scene is the chapter's next teaching.",
  "13": "\"But when Our enlightening signs came to them, they said,\"\"This is pure magic\" records the denial, and the verse is the teaching on the naming, and the teaching is the verse's content. The verse records the denial: \"But when Our enlightening signs came to them, they said, \"This is pure magic,\"\" and the word \"enlightening\" is 'mubsirah,' and the verse names the signs the enlightening, and the verse then records the naming of the magic, and the commentators explain the meaning: the enlightening signs were named the magic by the rebellious, and the naming is the verse's first stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the naming, and the teaching is that the truth is named the magic by the one who cannot answer, and the verse then closes, and the closing is the verse's second stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's design, and the design is the next verse's subject, and the subject is the conviction, and the conviction is the chapter's next teaching. The verse is the chapter's word on the naming, and the word is the verse's last stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the pattern, and the teaching is that the naming of the magic is the denial's standing word, and the word is the chapter's word. The denial is the chapter's picture of the scene's turn, and the turn is the teaching, and the teaching is that the enlightening signs were met with the pure magic's name, and the meeting is the chapter's word, and the word carries the story to its verdict, and the verdict is the chapter's next teaching. The naming of the magic is the denial's word, and the word teaches that the enlightening signs were named the magic by the one who cannot answer. The naming of the magic is the denial's word, and the word teaches that the enlightening signs were named the magic by the one who cannot answer, and the naming is the pattern.",
  "14": "\"And, although their hearts were convinced the signs were true, they still denied them wrongfully and arrogantly. See then what was the end of the corruptors!\" states the verdict, and the verse is the teaching on the conviction, and the teaching is the verse's content. The verse states the verdict: \"And, although their hearts were convinced the signs were true, they still denied them wrongfully and arrogantly. See then what was the end of the corruptors!\" and the word \"convinced\" is 'istayqanat,' and the verse records the hearts' conviction and the tongues' denial, and the commentators explain the meaning: the denial of the convinced is the denial of the pride, and the pride is the verse's first stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the conviction, and the teaching is that the known truth and the refused truth are the corruptor's two, and the verse then states the end: \"See then what was the end of the corruptors!\" and the end is the verse's second stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the verdict, and the teaching is that the corruptor's end is the known, and the verse then closes, and the closing is the verse's last stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's design, and the design is the next verse's subject, and the subject is the story of Solomon, and the story is the chapter's next teaching. The verse is the chapter's word on the conviction, and the word is the chapter's teaching on the disease, and the teaching is that the wrongful and the arrogant are the denial's two marks, and the marks are the chapter's word, and the word carries the chapter to its next story, and the story is the chapter's next teaching. The conviction and the denial are the corruptor's two, and the two teach that the known truth and the refused truth are the disease of the pride.",
  "15": "\"Indeed, We granted knowledge to David and Solomon. And they said ˹in acknowledgment˺,\"\"All praise is for Allah Who has privileged us over many of His faithful servants\" opens the story of Solomon, and the verse is the teaching on the knowledge, and the teaching is the verse's content. The verse states the gift: \"Indeed, We granted knowledge to David and Solomon. And they said ˹in acknowledgment˺, \"All praise is for Allah Who has privileged us over many of His faithful servants,\"\" and the word \"knowledge\" is 'ilman,' and the verse records the two gifts, and the commentators explain the meaning: the knowledge of the two prophets is the gift, and the praise is the acknowledgment, and the two are the verse's first stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the knowledge, and the teaching is that the gift of the knowledge is met with the praise, and the verse then closes, and the closing is the verse's second stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's design, and the design is the next verse's subject, and the subject is the succession, and the succession is the chapter's next teaching. The verse is the chapter's word on the knowledge, and the word is the verse's last stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the manner, and the teaching is that the gifted prophet turns the gift into the praise, and the turning is the chapter's word. The acknowledgment is the chapter's picture of the prophet's humility, and the humility is the teaching, and the teaching is that the privilege over the many is the praise of the One, and the One is the chapter's word, and the word opens the story of Solomon, and the story is the chapter's next teaching. The acknowledgment of the praise is the prophet's humility, and the humility teaches that the privilege over the many is the praise of the One.",
  "16": "\"And David was succeeded by Solomon, who said,\"\"O people! We have been taught the language of birds, and been given everything ˹we need˺. This is indeed a great privilege\" continues the story, and the verse is the teaching on the succession, and the teaching is the verse's content. The verse states the succession: \"And David was succeeded by Solomon, who said, \"O people! We have been taught the language of birds, and been given everything ˹we need˺. This is indeed a great privilege,\"\" and the word \"succeeded\" is 'waritha,' and the verse records the inheritance of the knowledge, and the commentators explain the meaning: Solomon inherited the prophecy and the knowledge, and the verse then records his announcement of the bird's language and the great privilege, and the announcement is the verse's first stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the succession, and the teaching is that the gift of the father passed to the son, and the verse then closes, and the closing is the verse's second stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's design, and the design is the next verse's subject, and the subject is the forces, and the forces are the chapter's next teaching. The verse is the chapter's word on the succession, and the word is the verse's last stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the gratitude, and the teaching is that the announced privilege is the praised, and the praised is the chapter's word. The announcement is the chapter's picture of the gift's scope, and the scope is the teaching, and the teaching is that the language of the birds and the everything are the two gifts, and the gifts are the chapter's word, and the word carries the story to its scene, and the scene is the chapter's next teaching.",
  "17": "\"Solomon’s forces of jinn, humans, and birds were rallied for him, perfectly organized\" states the forces states the forces, and the verse is the teaching on the gathering, and the teaching is the verse's content. The verse states the forces: \"Solomon’s forces of jinn, humans, and birds were rallied for him, perfectly organized\", and the word \"forces\" is 'junud,' and the verse names the three, and the commentators explain the meaning: the jinn and the humans and the birds are the three armies of Solomon, and the organizing is the verse's first stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the gathering, and the teaching is that the gift of Solomon gathered the three kinds into the one order, and the verse then closes, and the closing is the verse's second stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's design, and the design is the next verse's subject, and the subject is the ants, and the ants are the chapter's next teaching. The verse is the chapter's word on the gathering, and the word is the verse's last stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the order, and the teaching is that the perfectly organized is the gift's sign, and the sign is the chapter's word. The three forces are the chapter's picture of the kingdom's scope, and the scope is the teaching, and the teaching is that the three kinds in the one order are the Lord's subjection, and the subjection is the chapter's word, and the word carries the story to its great scene, and the scene is the chapter's next teaching. The three forces are the kingdom's scope, and the scope teaches that the jinn and the humans and the birds are the one order, for the subjection is the Lord's gift to Solomon. The verse is the chapter's word on the gathering, and the gathering teaches that the perfectly organized is the gift's sign, for the three kinds in the one order are the Lord's subjection, and the subjection is the chapter's picture of the kingdom's scope.",
  "18": "\"And when they came across a valley of ants, an ant warned,\"\"O ants! Go quickly into your homes so Solomon and his armies do not crush you, unknowingly\" records the ants, and the verse is the teaching on the ant, and the teaching is the verse's content. The verse records the scene: \"And when they came across a valley of ants, an ant warned, \"O ants! Go quickly into your homes so Solomon and his armies do not crush you, unknowingly,\"\" and the word \"valley\" is 'wadi,' and the verse records the ant's warning, and the commentators explain the meaning: the ant's word is the chapter's sign of the bird-language's truth, and the word is the verse's first stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the ant, and the teaching is that the smallest creature's warning was heard by the prophet, and the verse then closes, and the closing is the verse's second stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's design, and the design is the next verse's subject, and the subject is the smile, and the smile is the chapter's next teaching. The verse is the chapter's word on the ant, and the word is the verse's last stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the justice, and the teaching is that the ant's warning named the unknowing, and the unknowing is the chapter's word. The scene is the chapter's picture of the gift's wonder, and the wonder is the teaching, and the teaching is that the hearing of the ant is the hearing of the Lord's gift, and the gift is the chapter's word, and the word carries the story to the prophet's response, and the response is the chapter's next teaching. The ant's warning is the gift's wonder, and the wonder teaches that the hearing of the smallest creature is the hearing of the Lord's gift.",
  "19": "\"So Solomon smiled in amusement at her words, and prayed,\"\"My Lord! Inspire me to ˹always˺ be thankful for Your favours which You have blessed me and my parents with, and to do good deeds that please you. Admit me, by Your mercy, into ˹the company of˺ Your righteous servants\" records the prayer, and the verse is the teaching on the gratitude, and the teaching is the verse's content. The verse records the scene: \"So Solomon smiled in amusement at her words, and prayed, \"My Lord! Inspire me to ˹always˺ be thankful for Your favours which You have blessed me and my parents with, and to do good deeds that please you. Admit me, by Your mercy, into ˹the company of˺ Your righteous servants,\"\" and the word \"smiled\" is 'tabassama,' and the verse records the smile and the prayer, and the commentators explain the meaning: the smile is the joy of the gift, and the prayer is the gratitude of the gift, and the two are the verse's first stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the gratitude, and the teaching is that the wonder of the ant turned into the prayer of the thanks, and the verse then closes, and the closing is the verse's second stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's design, and the design is the next verse's subject, and the subject is the inspection, and the inspection is the chapter's next teaching. The verse is the chapter's word on the gratitude, and the word is the verse's last stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the manner, and the teaching is that the prophet's response to the wonder is the asking of the thanks and the good and the company, and the three askings are the chapter's word. The prayer is the chapter's picture of the believer's response, and the response is the teaching, and the teaching is that the smile and the prayer are the one heart's two faces, and the faces are the chapter's word, and the word carries the story to its next scene, and the scene is the chapter's next teaching.",
  "20": "\"˹One day˺ he inspected the birds, and wondered,\"\"Why is it that I cannot see the hoopoe? Or could he be absent?\" continues the story, and the verse is the teaching on the inspection, and the teaching is the verse's content. The verse records the inspection: \"˹One day˺ he inspected the birds, and wondered, \"Why is it that I cannot see the hoopoe? Or could he be absent?\"\" and the word \"hoopoe\" is 'hudhud,' and the verse records the missing bird, and the commentators explain the meaning: the inspection of the birds is the kingdom's order, and the missing hoopoe is the story's turn, and the two are the verse's first stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the inspection, and the teaching is that the prophet's kingdom is the ordered, and the verse then leaves the sentence to the next verse, and the leaving is the verse's second stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's design, and the design is the next verse's subject, and the subject is the threat, and the threat is the chapter's next teaching. The verse is the chapter's word on the inspection, and the word is the verse's last stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the story, and the teaching is that the missing bird is the story's hinge, and the hinge is the chapter's word. The scene is the chapter's picture of the kingdom's care, and the care is the teaching, and the teaching is that the ordered kingdom notices the one absence, and the noticing is the chapter's word, and the word carries the story to its turning, and the turning is the chapter's next teaching. The missing hoopoe is the story's hinge, and the hinge teaches that the ordered kingdom notices the one absence, and the noticing is the story's turn.",
  "21": "\"I will surely subject him to a severe punishment, or ˹even˺ slaughter him, unless he brings me a compelling excuse\"\" continues the story, and the verse is the teaching on the threat, and the teaching is the verse's content. The verse continues: \"I will surely subject him to a severe punishment, or ˹even˺ slaughter him, unless he brings me a compelling excuse,\"\" and the word \"excuse\" is 'sultan,' and the verse records the threat and the door, and the commentators explain the meaning: the prophet's threat is the kingdom's order, and the door of the excuse is the justice, and the two are the verse's first stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the threat, and the teaching is that the severe and the slaughter are the absence's two ends, and the excuse is the absence's one door, and the verse then closes, and the closing is the verse's second stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's design, and the design is the next verse's subject, and the subject is the bird's return, and the return is the chapter's next teaching. The verse is the chapter's word on the threat, and the word is the verse's last stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the balance, and the teaching is that the ordered kingdom's justice has the severity and the door, and the two are the chapter's word. The threat is the chapter's picture of the story's setup, and the setup is the teaching, and the teaching is that the threatened bird returns with the news, and the news is the chapter's word, and the word carries the story to its great turn, and the turn is the chapter's next teaching. The threat and the door are the kingdom's justice, and the justice teaches that the severity and the excuse are the one order's two faces.",
  "22": "\"It was not long before the bird came and said,\"\"I have found out something you do not know. I have just come to you from Sheba with sure news\" records the return, and the verse is the teaching on the news, and the teaching is the verse's content. The verse records the return: \"It was not long before the bird came and said, \"I have found out something you do not know. I have just come to you from Sheba with sure news,\"\" and the word \"Sheba\" is 'saba',' and the verse records the bird's claim, and the commentators explain the meaning: the hoopoe returned with the news of Sheba, and the returning is the verse's first stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the news, and the teaching is that the bird's news is the story's turn, and the verse then leaves the sentence to the next verse, and the leaving is the verse's second stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's design, and the design is the next verse's subject, and the subject is the queen, and the queen is the chapter's next teaching. The verse is the chapter's word on the news, and the word is the verse's last stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the wonder, and the teaching is that the smallest messenger carried the greatest news, and the carrying is the chapter's word. The return is the chapter's picture of the story's hinge, and the hinge is the teaching, and the teaching is that the sure news of the bird is the chapter's introduction of the queen, and the introduction is the chapter's word, and the word carries the story to its scene, and the scene is the chapter's next teaching. The bird's news is the story's turn, and the turn teaches that the smallest messenger carried the greatest news, and the carrying is the gift's wonder.",
  "23": "\"Indeed, I found a woman ruling over them, who has been given everything ˹she needs˺, and who has a magnificent throne\" continues the news, and the verse is the teaching on the queen, and the teaching is the verse's content. The verse continues: \"Indeed, I found a woman ruling over them, who has been given everything ˹she needs˺, and who has a magnificent throne\", and the word \"ruling\" is 'tamlikuhum,' and the verse records the queen and her two gifts, and the commentators explain the meaning: the queen of Sheba and her everything and her throne are the news's content, and the content is the verse's first stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the queen, and the teaching is that the bird's news described the ruler and her wealth, and the verse then leaves the sentence to the next verse, and the leaving is the verse's second stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's design, and the design is the next verse's subject, and the subject is the prostration, and the prostration is the chapter's next teaching. The verse is the chapter's word on the queen, and the word is the verse's last stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the measure, and the teaching is that the everything and the magnificent throne are the world's two gifts, and the gifts are the chapter's word. The description is the chapter's picture of the story's setup, and the setup is the teaching, and the teaching is that the queen of the everything worshipped the sun, and the worshipping is the chapter's next verse's word, and the word carries the story to its heart, and the heart is the chapter's next teaching. The queen and her two gifts are the news's content, and the content teaches that the everything and the magnificent throne are the world's two gifts.",
  "24": "\"I found her and her people prostrating to the sun instead of Allah. For Satan has made their deeds appealing to them—hindering them from the ˹Right˺ Way and leaving them unguided—\" continues the news, and the verse is the teaching on the prostration, and the teaching is the verse's content. The verse continues: \"I found her and her people prostrating to the sun instead of Allah. For Satan has made their deeds appealing to them—hindering them from the ˹Right˺ Way and leaving them unguided—\" and the word \"prostrating\" is 'yasjudun,' and the verse records the sun-worship, and the commentators explain the meaning: the queen of the everything worshipped the sun, and the worshipping is the verse's first stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the prostration, and the teaching is that the world's two gifts and the sun's prostration are the one people, and the verse then states the cause: \"Satan has made their deeds appealing to them\", and the cause is the verse's second stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the appealing, and the teaching is that the beautified deed is the devil's hindering, and the verse then leaves the sentence to the next verse, and the leaving is the verse's last stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's design, and the design is the next verse's subject, and the subject is the hidden, and the hidden is the chapter's next teaching. The verse is the chapter's word on the prostration, and the word is the chapter's teaching on the scene, and the teaching is that the bird's news named the sin and its cause, and the naming is the chapter's word, and the word carries the story to its next stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's next teaching. The prostration to the sun is the news's heart, and the heart teaches that the world's two gifts and the sun's prostration are the one people.",
  "25": "\"so they do not prostrate to Allah, Who brings forth what is hidden in the heavens and the earth, and knows what you ˹all˺ conceal and what you reveal\" continues the news, and the verse is the teaching on the hidden, and the teaching is the verse's content. The verse continues: \"so they do not prostrate to Allah, Who brings forth what is hidden in the heavens and the earth, and knows what you ˹all˺ conceal and what you reveal\", and the word \"hidden\" is 'khab',' and the verse records the true God's description, and the commentators explain the meaning: the One who brings forth the hidden is the One who deserves the prostration, and the describing is the verse's first stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the hidden, and the teaching is that the sun is the creature and the Lord is the bringer-forth, and the verse then leaves the sentence to the next verse, and the leaving is the verse's second stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's design, and the design is the next verse's subject, and the subject is the creed, and the creed is the chapter's next teaching. The verse is the chapter's word on the hidden, and the word is the verse's last stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the contrast, and the teaching is that the worshipped sun and the worshipped Lord are the two, and the two are the chapter's word. The description is the chapter's picture of the news's heart, and the heart is the teaching, and the teaching is that the bird's news carried the creed, and the creed is the chapter's word, and the word carries the story to its declaration, and the declaration is the chapter's next teaching. The bringing-forth of the hidden is the true God's description, and the description teaches that the worshipped sun and the worshipped Lord are the two.",
  "26": "\"˹He is˺ Allah! There is no god ˹worthy of worship˺ except Him, the Lord of the Mighty Throne\"\" completes the news, and the verse is the teaching on the creed, and the teaching is the verse's content. The verse completes the news: \"˹He is˺ Allah! There is no god ˹worthy of worship˺ except Him, the Lord of the Mighty Throne,\"\" and the word \"Mighty Throne\" is 'arsh al-azim,' and the verse records the creed, and the commentators explain the meaning: the hoopoe's news ended with the oneness, and the ending is the verse's first stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the creed, and the teaching is that the bird carried the chapter's standing creed to the prophet, and the verse then closes, and the closing is the verse's second stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's design, and the design is the next verse's subject, and the subject is the testing, and the testing is the chapter's next teaching. The verse is the chapter's word on the creed, and the word is the verse's last stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the wonder, and the teaching is that the creed of the oneness was spoken by the smallest of the forces, and the speaking is the chapter's word. The declaration is the chapter's picture of the news's close, and the close is the teaching, and the teaching is that the Lord of the Mighty Throne is the one God, and the one is the chapter's word, and the word carries the story to Solomon's answer, and the answer is the chapter's next teaching. The creed of the oneness is the news's close, and the close teaches that the bird carried the chapter's standing creed to the prophet. The creed of the oneness is the news's close, and the close teaches that the bird carried the chapter's standing creed, for the smallest of the forces spoke the greatest of the words.",
  "27": "Solomon said, \"We will see whether you are telling the truth or lying\" records the testing, and the verse is the teaching on the verification, and the teaching is the verse's content. The verse records the answer: \"Solomon said, \"We will see whether you are telling the truth or lying,\"\" and the word \"see\" is 'sananzur,' and the verse records the verification, and the commentators explain the meaning: the prophet did not accept the news without the test, and the testing is the verse's first stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the verification, and the teaching is that the truth is tested before it is acted on, and the verse then leaves the sentence to the next verse, and the leaving is the verse's second stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's design, and the design is the next verse's subject, and the subject is the letter, and the letter is the chapter's next teaching. The verse is the chapter's word on the verification, and the word is the verse's last stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the manner, and the teaching is that the kingdom's justice verifies the claim, and the verifying is the chapter's word. The testing is the chapter's picture of the story's turn, and the turn is the teaching, and the teaching is that the verified news became the letter, and the letter is the chapter's word, and the word carries the story to its next scene, and the scene is the chapter's next teaching. The verification of the news is the prophet's manner, and the manner teaches that the truth is tested before it is acted on. The verification of the news is the prophet's manner, and the manner teaches that the truth is tested before it is acted on, for the verified claim is the letter's ground.",
  "28": "\"Go with this letter of mine and deliver it to them, then stand aside and see how they will respond\"\" continues the story, and the verse is the teaching on the letter, and the teaching is the verse's content. The verse continues: \"Go with this letter of mine and deliver it to them, then stand aside and see how they will respond,\"\" and the word \"letter\" is 'kitabi,' and the verse records the sending, and the commentators explain the meaning: the letter to the queen is the story's great turn, and the turning is the verse's first stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the letter, and the teaching is that the prophet's word reached the queen before the army, and the verse then closes, and the closing is the verse's second stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's design, and the design is the next verse's subject, and the subject is the announcement, and the announcement is the chapter's next teaching. The verse is the chapter's word on the letter, and the word is the verse's last stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the method, and the teaching is that the word before the force is the mercy's order, and the order is the chapter's word. The sending is the chapter's picture of the story's hinge, and the hinge is the teaching, and the teaching is that the delivered letter and the standing bird are the mission's two, and the two are the chapter's word, and the word carries the story to the queen's court, and the court is the chapter's next teaching. The letter before the army is the mercy's order, and the order teaches that the word before the force is the prophet's method. The letter before the army is the mercy's order, and the order teaches that the word before the force is the prophet's method, for the delivered letter is the mission's first step.",
  "29": "\"The Queen ˹later˺ announced,\"\"O chiefs! Indeed, a noble letter has been delivered to me\" opens the queen's scene, and the verse is the teaching on the announcement, and the teaching is the verse's content. The verse records the announcement: \"The Queen ˹later˺ announced, \"O chiefs! Indeed, a noble letter has been delivered to me,\"\" and the word \"noble\" is 'karim,' and the verse records the queen's word, and the commentators explain the meaning: the queen announced the letter to her chiefs, and the announcing is the verse's first stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the announcement, and the teaching is that the letter's nobility is the queen's first reading, and the verse then leaves the sentence to the next verse, and the leaving is the verse's second stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's design, and the design is the next verse's subject, and the subject is the counsel, and the counsel is the chapter's next teaching. The verse is the chapter's word on the announcement, and the word is the verse's last stroke, and the stroke is the chapter's teaching on the scene, and the teaching is that the queen's court is the story's next stage, and the stage is the chapter's word. The announcement is the chapter's picture of the story's turn, and the turn is the teaching, and the teaching is that the noble letter of Solomon reached the sun-worshipping court, and the reaching is the chapter's word, and the word carries the story to its counsel, and the counsel is the chapter's next teaching. The announcement of the noble letter is the queen's first reading, and the reading teaches that the letter's nobility opened the court's scene. The announcement of the noble letter is the queen's first reading, and the reading teaches that the letter's nobility opened the court's scene, for the noble word is the scene's key.",
  "30": "\"It is from Solomon\" is how the queen continues her report after calling the letter \"a noble letter.\" She does not hide the sender. She names him at once, so her chiefs know that this is not a vague message from an unknown court. The wording is short and clear. The letter has an author, and that author is Solomon, the prophet-king whose forces of jinn, humans, and birds were already described earlier in the chapter.\n\nThe next words, \"and it reads,\" show that she is now quoting the letter itself. She is not summarizing it in her own style. She is placing Solomon's opening before her council so they can hear the same beginning she heard. Early commentators noted that this is a mark of a careful ruler. She does not twist the wording before asking for advice.\n\nWhat the letter first says is, \"In the Name of Allah—the Most Compassionate, Most Merciful.\" The Arabic formula behind this English line is \"bismillah al-Rahman al-Rahim.\" It is the same opening that begins the Quran itself and that Muslims still place at the start of important speech and writing. Solomon does not begin with his own titles. He does not list his armies or his throne. He begins with the Name of Allah. That choice already answers the hoopoe's report that the people of Sheba were prostrating to the sun. The letter's first line turns their worship away from the sun and toward the One who created the sun.\n\n\"The Most Compassionate, Most Merciful\" are not empty ornaments. They tell the queen that the God Solomon serves is not a local idol and not a harsh tyrant. The two names \"al-Rahman\" and \"al-Rahim\" both come from mercy, yet they are not the same. The first points to a wide mercy that covers all creation. The second points to a special mercy for those who turn to Allah. A letter that starts this way is already an invitation, not only a demand.\n\nScholars have long pointed out that this is one of the clearest places in the Quran where the \"basmalah\" is shown as a living practice of a prophet, not only as a heading of a chapter. Solomon writes as a believer. His politics begin with worship. Later verses will show that he refuses gifts and seeks submission to Allah, not a trade in gold. The seed of that later stand is already here, in the Name with which the letter opens.\n\nThe queen's court worshipped the sun, as the hoopoe said. Hearing \"In the Name of Allah\" must have sounded strange and serious. She still called the letter noble. That word from the previous verse and this verse belong together. Nobility, in her reading, includes the sender's name and the sacred opening. She has not yet submitted, but she has already recognized that this writing is not cheap flattery and not a common threat.",
  "31": "\"Do not be arrogant with me\" is the first command inside Solomon's letter after the Name of Allah. The queen is still quoting. The letter does not begin with war terms. It begins with a warning against pride. The Arabic sense behind \"do not be arrogant\" is \"do not exalt yourselves above me,\" meaning do not meet this call with haughtiness. Solomon is a king, yet he does not ask them to bow to his person as if he were a god. He asks them not to let pride block the truth.\n\nThe second half, \"but come to me, fully submitting to Allah,\" states the real goal. The key word behind \"fully submitting\" is \"muslimin.\" Here it does not first mean joining a later community by that name. It means coming in surrender to Allah, in peace and obedience. Solomon is not shopping for a treaty of gold. He is calling a sun-worshipping kingdom to the same Lord named in the letter's opening.\n\nThe two parts belong together. Arrogance is the barrier. Submission is the door. If they come while still puffed up, they have not answered the letter. If they come submitting to Allah, they have answered it. The previous verse placed Allah's Name first. This verse shows what that Name requires: leave pride, and come as servants of the Most Merciful.\n\nA reader might think Solomon is only demanding political surrender to his throne. The wording does not support that reading alone. He says \"fully submitting to Allah,\" not \"become my slaves.\" Later, when gifts arrive, he will refuse wealth and speak of Allah's greater grant. The letter and that later scene explain each other. His mission is prophetic, even though he is also a king with armies.\n\nThe queen has now read the whole short letter to her chiefs. It has a sender, a sacred opening, a ban on arrogance, and a call to come in submission. Nothing in it is long. Nothing in it is vague. That brevity is itself a kind of strength. A proud court might have expected flattery or a list of terms. Instead they hear a straight religious summons. The next verses will show how she asks for counsel, how her chiefs offer military power, and how she chooses a wiser first step than war. The letter has already set the measure by which every later choice will be judged.",
  "32": "\"She said, 'O chiefs! Advise me in this matter of mine'\" shows the queen turning from reading the letter to seeking counsel. She uses the same address, \"O chiefs,\" that she used when she first announced the noble letter. The court is not a decoration. She wants their judgment on \"this matter of mine,\" meaning the letter and the demand it carries. She does not pretend the affair is small.\n\nThe next clause, \"for I would never make any decision without you,\" explains her habit of rule. She is not claiming that she has no authority. Later they will say the decision is hers. She is saying that she does not cut a major matter alone. The Arabic idea behind \"I would never make any decision\" is that she does not cut an affair until they are present as witnesses and advisers. Early commentators praised this as political wisdom. A ruler who hears a grave letter and then hides it, or who answers it in secret, is not the picture given here.\n\nThis is not weakness. A weak ruler begs because she cannot act. This queen invites counsel because she understands that war, tribute, or travel to Solomon will touch the whole land. The hoopoe had said she was given everything she needed and had a magnificent throne. Wealth did not make her careless. She still gathers the chiefs.\n\nSome readers imagine that the Quran mentions her only to mock a woman on a throne. The wording does the opposite. It shows a thoughtful sovereign who reads carefully, names the sender honestly, and refuses to decide a nation-changing letter in isolation. The next verse will show that her chiefs are ready for war. Her later choice will not be their first impulse. The seeds of that independence are already here: she asks, but she is not bound to the loudest voice.\n\nThe letter said, \"Do not be arrogant.\" Asking for advice is itself a refusal of a certain kind of arrogance, the arrogance of a ruler who thinks no one else should speak. At the same time, counsel is not the same as surrender to Allah. She is still deciding as a queen of Sheba, not yet as a believer. The story holds both truths at once. She is wise in worldly rule, and she is not yet guided in worship. The coming verses will test which of those two will lead.",
  "33": "\"They responded, 'We are a people of strength and great military might'\" is the chiefs' first answer. They do not begin with the letter's theology. They begin with force. They want her to know that Sheba is not helpless. If Solomon's message is a threat, they can meet a threat. The phrase \"great military might\" is their pride of arms. In many courts, that is the sentence a ruler is expected to love.\n\nThen they add, \"but the decision is yours.\" They hand the choice back to her. They are strong, yet they do not seize the throne's right to command. \"So decide what you will command\" completes the picture. They will fight if she orders fighting. They will wait if she orders waiting. The army is ready. The judgment is hers.\n\nThis reply explains why she asked them. She needed to know whether the land still had power, and whether that power would obey her. Their answer is yes on both counts. A lesser story might have stopped there, as if strength were the whole reply to a prophet's letter. The Quran lets their boast stand, then lets the queen speak a deeper fear in the next verse. Force is available. Force is not the only wisdom.\n\nThere is a quiet contrast with Solomon's own court. When Solomon later asks who can bring her throne, his chiefs include a mighty jinn and a man of knowledge. Power and knowledge both speak. Here, only power speaks. No chief in this verse mentions the Name of Allah that opened the letter. No one asks whether the sun they worship can answer Solomon's Lord. The gap is the point. A nation can be rich, armed, and still spiritually unprepared.\n\nThe queen hears that she is not trapped. That freedom matters. If she later sends a gift instead of an army, it is not because she had no soldiers. It is because she chose another test. The chiefs have done their part. They have told her the sword is sharp. What she does with that news will show the kind of mind the Quran is holding up in this scene.",
  "34": "\"She reasoned, 'Indeed, when kings invade a land, they ruin it'\" is not a proverb said for decoration. It is her reading of history. She has heard her chiefs offer strength. She answers with what invasion actually does. Kings who enter a town as conquerors do not leave it as they found it. \"They ruin it\" means they spoil its order, its wealth, and its peace.\n\nShe continues, \"and debase its nobles.\" The high people of a conquered land are brought low. Thrones are overturned. Honor is stripped. She knows this because she is herself a noble on a magnificent throne. If Solomon is only another king, her chiefs' military pride may end in the very humiliation they hope to avoid.\n\nThen she seals the thought: \"They really do so!\" She is not guessing. She is stating a pattern. Commentators have noted that she speaks like someone who has watched the ways of power. She does not romanticize war. She does not treat her army's readiness as a reason to rush.\n\nA listener might think she is accusing Solomon of being a mere plunderer. The verse does not yet judge Solomon. It judges kings as a class. She is applying a worldly rule to a letter she has not fully understood. That is both wise and limited. Wise, because she refuses a careless war. Limited, because Solomon's letter asked for submission to Allah, not for a chance to loot Sheba.\n\nThe connection with the previous verse is tight. They said, we have might. She says, might used in invasion ruins lands and humiliates nobles. She is not rejecting their strength. She is refusing to spend it blindly. The next verse will show her chosen middle path: a gift, and a watch on the response. Before that tactic comes this moral memory. She will not be the queen who burned her own city in order to feel brave.\n\nThe Quran often warns that corruption follows arrogant power, as later in this same chapter the homes of Thamud stand ruined because of wrongdoing. Her sentence about invading kings sits in that larger moral world, even if she herself does not yet worship Allah.",
  "35": "\"But I will certainly send him a gift\" is the queen's decision after weighing war. The word \"but\" turns away from invasion as a first step. She will not march, and she will not ignore the letter. She will test Solomon with a present. Early reports in the tafsir tradition say the gift was rich, meant to see whether he was a king who could be bought or a prophet who could not. The Quran itself does not list the items. It names the act: she will send a gift.\n\nThe second half, \"and see what response my envoys will return with,\" shows her method. She does not only give. She watches. The envoys are her eyes. If Solomon accepts the gift and softens, she will know he is a king among kings. If he refuses and holds to his call, she will know the letter was serious. The hoopoe had brought her \"sure news.\" She now seeks sure news of her own.\n\nThis is the same careful mind that would not decide without the chiefs. She uses their strength as a reserve, not as a first word. She uses wealth, which she has in plenty, as a probe. Some may call this trickery. The verse presents it as deliberation. She is trying to read the man who wrote in the Name of Allah.\n\nThere is a risk in her plan. A gift can look like a bribe, and Solomon will treat it that way. A gift can also look like honor, and she may have meant honor as well as a test. The Quran lets both possibilities live until his reply. What is already clear is that she prefers knowledge before blood. That preference does not yet equal faith, but it keeps the door of the story open. If she had chosen war at once, the later scene of her throne and her submission would not unfold as it does.\n\nThe chiefs offered \"great military might.\" She offers a gift and a waiting eye. Between those two answers, the Quran is teaching something about leadership. Power is not wisdom. The next verses leave her court and return to Solomon, where the gift will meet a prophet who already has \"everything we need\" from Allah.",
  "36": "\"When the chief-envoy came to him\" moves the story from Sheba to Solomon's presence. The gift has arrived. The queen's test is now in the prophet's hands. Solomon does not admire the present. He speaks at once: \"Do you offer me wealth?\" The question is almost astonished. They have treated a call to Allah as if it were a bargain over money.\n\nHe explains why the offer fails: \"What Allah has granted me is far greater than what He has granted you.\" The comparison is not between two treasuries of gold. It is between Allah's gift to a prophet-king and the worldly plenty of a sun-worshipping court. Solomon had already said earlier that he had been taught the language of birds and given everything he needed, and he called that a great privilege. This verse returns to that same gratitude. Wealth from men cannot outbid wealth from Allah.\n\nThen comes the sharp line: \"No! It is you who rejoice in receiving gifts.\" He turns the gift back upon the givers. They are the ones who love presents. They measure honor by what the hand can hold. He will not play that game. The Arabic bite in the sentence is that their joy is in the gift itself, not in truth.\n\nA common mistake is to hear this as scorn for generosity. The Quran elsewhere praises giving. What Solomon rejects is using gifts to dodge a religious summons. The letter said, come submitting to Allah. The gift says, perhaps we can settle this with treasure. He refuses the substitution.\n\nThe scene also protects his later actions from a wrong reading. When he asks for her throne, it will not be because he covets furniture. When he warns of forces, it will not be because the gift was too small. He has already declared that Allah's grant is greater. The envoy must carry that answer home. The queen wanted to see what response her messengers would return with. This is the response: we are not for sale.",
  "37": "\"Go back to them\" is Solomon's order to the envoy after refusing the gift. The mission of the present is over. The messengers are not to linger as if a second bargain might work. They must return with a clear word.\n\nThat word is severe: \"for we will certainly mobilize against them forces which they can never resist.\" Solomon is not bluffing with an empty court. The chapter has already shown his rallied forces of jinn, humans, and birds, perfectly organized. The queen's chiefs boasted of strength and great military might. He answers that there are forces they cannot stand against. The warning is meant to end the illusion that Sheba can treat the letter as a market talk.\n\nHe adds, \"and we will drive them out from there in disgrace, fully humbled.\" The two phrases pile shame upon removal. If they persist in pride, they will not keep their land in honor. The queen had said that kings ruin a land and debase its nobles. Solomon's warning sounds, on the surface, like that very pattern. The difference is the cause. He is not hunting spoil. He is answering a rejected call to come \"fully submitting to Allah.\" The humility he mentions is the opposite of the arrogance the letter forbade.\n\nSome readers feel uneasy that a prophet speaks of armies. The Quran does not hide that unease away. It shows a prophet who first wrote in the Name of the Most Merciful, who first asked them to come in submission, who first refused their gold, and only then spoke of force. The order of the story matters. Mercy and argument came before the threat.\n\nThe envoy now has a harder report than a thank-you for gifts. The queen will learn that her test worked in one sense: she now knows Solomon is not a king who rejoices in presents. The next movement of the story will not wait for a battlefield. Solomon will ask for her throne to be brought before she arrives. The warning of forces and the coming sign will work together. He wants her to see power that is not like other kings' power.",
  "38": "\"Solomon asked, 'O chiefs!'\" turns from the departing envoys to his own council. He uses the same kind of address the queen used with her chiefs. Two courts are now in view. Hers offered military might and left the decision to her. His court is asked a stranger question: \"Which of you can bring me her throne before they come to me in full submission?\"\n\nThe throne was part of the hoopoe's first report: she has a magnificent throne. Solomon wants that throne present before the queen arrives as one submitting. The aim, as the next verses will show, is not theft for its own sake. It is a sign. If her unique throne appears in his court before she does, she will know that the God who sent the letter is not like the sun she worshipped.\n\n\"Before they come to me in full submission\" keeps the letter's goal in view. He still expects them to come as \"muslimin,\" people submitting to Allah. The throne is to be there first, so that the coming is met by a miracle, not by a pile of returned gifts.\n\nScholars have discussed why the throne was chosen. It was the symbol of her rule, the object most tied to her pride and identity. Changing it later, as he will command, becomes a test of recognition. None of that is spelled out in this verse alone. This verse only asks who can fetch it in time.\n\nThe question also displays the range of Solomon's kingdom. He does not send a human caravan on a long road. He asks a council that includes beings able to do what ordinary soldiers cannot. The next two speakers will make that clear: a mighty jinn, then one who had knowledge of the Scripture. Power will speak, then knowledge will speak faster. The queen's chiefs had only one kind of answer. Solomon's chiefs will show two, and the second will win.\n\nNothing here cancels his earlier gratitude. He is not collecting furniture to enlarge his glory. The following verses will put thanks on his tongue the moment he sees the throne. The request must be read in that light, or the story is pulled down to royal greed.",
  "39": "\"One mighty jinn responded\" introduces a creature from the forces already named in this chapter, the jinn rallied with humans and birds. The word behind \"mighty jinn\" is \"ifrit,\" used for a powerful, formidable being from among the jinn. He is not a random demon crashing the scene. He is part of Solomon's organized host, and he speaks as a servant of the prophet's command.\n\nHis offer is timed: \"I can bring it to you before you rise from this council of yours.\" The throne of Sheba would arrive before Solomon even stands up from the sitting. That is already far beyond human travel. The jinn measures the task by the length of a royal session, not by days of marching.\n\nHe adds a double credential: \"And I am quite strong and trustworthy for this task.\" Strength means he can lift and carry what is heavy and guarded. Trustworthiness means he will not steal its treasure or alter it for himself. A throne is covered with wealth. The jinn knows that the prophet must hear both qualities. Power without trust would be a danger. Trust without power could not move the throne.\n\nThe verse lets this offer stand as real. The Quran does not treat the jinn as a folk tale added for color. It treats him as a capable agent under Solomon's authority, the same authority given as a privilege from Allah. At the same time, the next verse will outrun him. Strength is not the last word in Solomon's court, just as military might was not the last word in the queen's court.\n\nA listener should not imagine that Muslims are being taught to seek ifrits for their own errands. The scene is tied to a prophet to whom Allah subjected the jinn. The lesson inside the story is the ranking of gifts: raw force offers speed, yet a higher gift will offer a blink. Solomon will not refuse the jinn with insult. He will simply receive a better offer. That calm ranking is itself part of his wisdom.",
  "40": "\"But the one who had knowledge of the Scripture said\" shifts the contest from muscle to knowledge. The Quran does not name this person here. Many early commentators said he was a righteous man from Solomon's court, often identified as Asif son of Barkhiya, though other views exist and the text itself is silent on the name. What the text stresses is the means: \"knowledge of the Scripture.\" The Arabic \"al-kitab\" points to revealed writing, not to a trick book. The power he claims is tied to knowledge Allah taught, not to the jinn's bodily force.\n\nHis promise is shorter than the ifrit's sitting: \"I can bring it to you in the blink of an eye.\" The phrase means before a glance returns, before the eye can open and close. The throne will not take the length of a council. It will take the length of a look.\n\n\"So when Solomon saw it placed before him\" shows the promise kept at once. There is no travel scene. The throne is simply there, settled in his presence. Solomon does not boast that his servant beat the jinn. He does not inspect the gold first. He exclaimed, \"This is by the grace of my Lord to test me whether I am grateful or ungrateful.\"\n\nThat sentence is the heart of the verse. The miracle is a test, \"li-yabluwani,\" to try him. Grace can make a person thankful, or it can make a person proud. Solomon chooses the first reading. He had already prayed, after hearing the ant, to be inspired to thanks. He lives that prayer again beside the throne.\n\nHe then states a rule: \"And whoever is grateful, it is only for their own good.\" Thanks does not enrich Allah. It benefits the thankful. \"But whoever is ungrateful, surely my Lord is Self-Sufficient, Most Generous.\" The names \"Ghani\" and \"Karim\" mean that Allah needs no one's thanks, and still He gives. Ingratitude cannot bankrupt Him. It only harms the ungrateful.\n\nThe queen had sent wealth to test Solomon. Allah sent a throne in a blink to test Solomon. He passed by turning the sign into praise. That is why this long verse spends more words on gratitude than on the method of the transfer. The method is a wonder. The thanks is the teaching.",
  "41": "\"Then Solomon said, 'Disguise her throne for her'\" begins a second test, not of himself this time, but of the queen. The throne is already present by Allah's grace. Now its appearance is to be altered. \"Disguise\" means change its features so that it is not obvious at first glance. The aim is stated at once: \"so we may see whether she will recognize it or she will not be able to.\"\n\nThe test is about recognition, \"a-tahtadi,\" whether she will be guided to the truth of what she sees. The same root of guidance has run through the hoopoe's report that her people were left unguided. Solomon wants to know if her mind is sharp and if her heart can move from familiar idolatry toward the truth.\n\nWhy disguise a throne she knows better than anyone? If she sees it unchanged, she might think it is a copy, or she might faint from shock, or she might argue. If she sees it altered and still senses, \"It looks to be the same,\" she shows a careful intelligence. Commentators have also said the test would reveal whether she is guided to the larger truth that Allah can do what her sun cannot do.\n\nThis is not a cruel game. The previous verse tied every wonder to gratitude and to a test from the Lord. The queen is being prepared, not mocked. She is on her way \"in full submission\" after the failed gift. Solomon meets her with a sign that speaks to the very emblem of her kingdom.\n\nSome may think he is toying with a guest. The wording is observational: \"so we may see.\" He is reading her as she once tried to read him with a gift. She tested whether he loved wealth. He tests whether she can recognize truth when it is near and slightly veiled. The next verse will record her cautious answer, neither reckless certainty nor blind denial. The disguise sets up that wise middle sentence.",
  "42": "\"So when she arrived, it was said to her, 'Is your throne like this?'\" The question is careful. It does not say, \"This is your throne.\" It asks if hers is like the one before her. The disguise has done its work. She must look, compare, and speak.\n\n\"She replied, 'It looks to be the same.'\" She neither denies nor claims a fool's certainty. The throne resembles hers so strongly that she will not say it is other, yet the changes keep her from a blunt \"yes.\" Commentators have long praised this answer as the speech of an intelligent woman. She sees what is before her eyes and does not go beyond it.\n\nThen she adds something larger than furniture: \"We have already received knowledge of Solomon's prophethood before this miracle, and have submitted to Allah.\" The gift's failure, the letter, and the reports of her envoys have already taught her. The throne confirms what she has begun to know. She uses \"we,\" speaking as a ruler whose circle has started to accept the truth. \"Submitted\" again carries the sense of \"muslimin,\" surrender to Allah, the same goal named in the letter.\n\nA reader might think the miracle alone created her faith. Her own words correct that. Knowledge came \"before this.\" The sign seals and strengthens. That order matches how the Quran often treats signs: they help those who already begin to see, and they do not force closed hearts.\n\nThere is still a further confession coming in the palace of crystal. This verse is an important middle step, not the final sentence of her story. She has recognized, she has claimed knowledge, she has spoken of submission. The next verse will explain what had blocked her before. The story is honest about both her insight and her past hindrance. Wisdom in a throne room is not yet the same as a full turning of the soul, but it is no small thing, and the Quran records it with respect.",
  "43": "\"But she had been hindered by what she used to worship instead of Allah\" looks back from her arriving words to the years behind them. The throne test showed a keen mind. This verse explains why such a mind had still bowed to the sun. She was \"hindered,\" blocked, turned aside. The object of that blocking was \"what she used to worship instead of Allah.\" False worship is not only a mistake in theory. It stands in the road.\n\nThe hoopoe had already said that Satan made their deeds appealing and hindered them from the Right Way. This verse applies that same hindrance to her personally. Custom, people, and beautified rites can hold even a thoughtful ruler. She was not foolish. She was raised inside a worship that claimed her loyalty.\n\nThe last clause, \"for she was indeed from a disbelieving people,\" places her in a community. Faith and unbelief are not only private moods. She belonged to a people who covered the truth, a \"kafirin\" people, and their way pressed upon her. The Quran often speaks this way: a person is influenced by the nation that surrounds them, yet is still responsible when the truth becomes clear.\n\nThis is not said to cancel her coming submission. It is said so that no one thinks sun-worship was a small side habit. It had real power to hinder. When she later says she has wronged her soul, this verse has already named the wrong: worship besides Allah, inherited from a disbelieving people.\n\nA misconception would be to hear the verse as a final stamp that she remained a disbeliever. The \"but\" looks at the past that delayed her, while the surrounding story moves her toward \"the Lord of all worlds.\" Another misconception would be to treat her people as an excuse that removes blame. The verse explains the hindrance. It does not praise it. Truth still had to cut through people, custom, and the appealing deeds of the sun cult. The next scene in the crystal palace is where that cutting becomes her own clear confession.",
  "44": "\"Then she was told, 'Enter the palace'\" opens the last public test of the queen. She is invited in, not driven in. \"But when she saw the hall, she thought it was a body of water, so she bared her legs.\" The floor was so clear that she took it for a pool. Lifting her garment to wade is the natural act of someone who believes she must cross water. The Quran mentions this without scorn. It is what any guest might do if the eyes are deceived by perfect craft.\n\n\"Solomon said. 'It is just a palace paved with crystal.'\" He corrects the sight at once. He does not leave her standing ashamed. The hall is \"sarh,\" a paved palace, made smooth with glass or crystal, \"qawarir.\" Human art, given to Solomon as part of his kingdom, has produced a surface that mimics water. The point is not luxury for display alone. She has now seen a throne travel in a way no carpenter of Sheba could match, and a floor that fools the eye. Her sun-god does none of this.\n\n\"At last she declared, 'My Lord! I have certainly wronged my soul.'\" The address is to her Lord, not first to Solomon. The wrong is against her own soul, the worship and delay named in the previous verse. She does not blame her chiefs. She does not blame the beautiful floor. She owns the injustice she did to herself by worshipping other than Allah.\n\n\"Now I fully submit myself along with Solomon to Allah, the Lord of all worlds.\" The submission is \"with Solomon,\" not to Solomon. She joins him in serving the same Lord. \"The Lord of all worlds\" lifts her off the local sun and onto the God of every world, the same title heard when Moses was called at the fire in this chapter. Her story and Moses' story meet in that Name.\n\nSome later tales add marriage details the Quran does not give. The verse ends where the guidance ends: a queen who once had a magnificent throne now has a magnificent confession. The gift failed. The letter stood. The signs taught. The hindrance broke. She enters Islam as the letter first asked, fully submitting to Allah.",
  "45": "\"And We certainly sent to the people of Thamud their brother Salih\" turns the chapter from Solomon's court to another prophet and another people. The word \"And\" continues the series of stories that prove Allah's sending of messengers. Thamud were an ancient Arabian people, often placed in the rocky lands of northwest Arabia. Salih is called \"their brother\" because he was from them, known to them, not a stranger with no share in their life.\n\nHis message is given in two words of meaning: \"Worship Allah.\" That is the same core Solomon wrote to Sheba and the same call every prophet carries. The Quran does not invent a new religion for each nation. It repeats the first duty: serve the one God.\n\n\"But they suddenly split into two opposing groups.\" The people did not stay one audience. They became two parties in dispute, \"yantasimoon,\" quarreling with each other. One group believed Salih. The other refused. Other passages about Thamud, such as the story of the she-camel, fill in more of their trial. This verse stresses the split itself. Guidance did not fail to arrive. The people broke over it.\n\nThe contrast with the queen of Sheba is instructive. She consulted and moved, however slowly, toward submission. Thamud fracture. A prophet can be \"their brother\" and still be opposed by his own kin. Family ties do not guarantee faith.\n\nThe verse is short, but it sets the whole coming scene: a divided city, a prophet urging mercy, a party that calls him a bad omen, and nine elite men who plot murder. None of that is visible yet. What is visible is the pattern. Allah sent. The message was worship. The result among Thamud was a quarrel, not a united thanks. The next verse will show Salih speaking to the disbelieving side of that quarrel, asking why they run toward torment instead of forgiveness.",
  "46": "\"He urged the disbelieving group, 'O my people!'\" keeps Salih inside the bond of peoplehood even while he corrects them. He does not call them strangers. He calls them \"my people,\" the same tenderness many prophets use when the wound is closest.\n\n\"Why do you seek to hasten the torment rather than grace?\" names their strange haste. They want the threatened punishment to come now, as if daring Allah, rather than wanting the good that would come if they believed. Other Quran passages show disbelievers saying, bring us what you promise if you are truthful. Salih hears that spirit and names it: you are rushing the bad before the good.\n\n\"If only you sought Allah's forgiveness so you may be shown mercy!\" is the path they will not take. Forgiveness, \"istighfar,\" is asking Allah to cover and remove sin. Mercy is what they would hope to receive after that asking. The sentence is a wish, \"if only,\" because they are not doing it. Salih's door is still open. He is not hungry for their destruction. He is hungry for their turning.\n\nThe split of the previous verse is now given a moral shape. One side would seek forgiveness. The other side hurries toward the very torment they claim not to believe. That contradiction is common. People mock a warning and then live as if they are eager to meet it.\n\nA misconception is to think prophets enjoy announcing doom. This verse will not allow that picture. The prophet of Thamud begs for istighfar and mercy. If ruin comes later, it will come after this plea, not instead of it. The next reply from his people will show how they answer mercy: they will call him and his followers a bad omen. The contrast between his wish and their superstition is about to become sharp.",
  "47": "\"They replied, 'You and your followers are a bad omen for us.'\" This is how the disbelieving party answers a call to seek forgiveness. They do not discuss worship. They blame misfortune on Salih and those with him. The Arabic \"ittayyarna\" comes from reading birds as signs, then came to mean seeing an evil omen. Whatever drought, split, or fear they felt, they pinned it on the prophet.\n\nSalih's answer is firm: \"Your omens are destined by Allah.\" The birds, the luck, the so-called signs they fear, are not in Salih's hand. They are with Allah, who creates the events they misread. Superstition steals events from Allah and hangs them on a human being. Salih gives the events back to their true Owner.\n\n\"In fact, you are only a people being tested.\" The word \"tuftanoon\" means they are under trial. Hardship and the prophet's presence are a test, not a curse from Salih. Tests reveal what is in a people. If they respond with blame and omens, the test has already shown their disease.\n\nThe Quran repeats this pattern with other prophets. Pharaoh's people blamed Moses for whatever struck them. Some of Mecca treated the Prophet Muhammad as a cause of division. The habit is old: when the heart will not face its own sin, it looks for a jinx.\n\nSalih had just offered forgiveness and mercy. They offered omen-talk. The two replies cannot be mixed. One treats Allah as the One who forgives. The other treats the messenger as a walking curse. The coming verses will show how far that hostility goes. Nine men in the city will not stop at words. They will swear to kill. Omen-speech, once it hardens, can become a plot. This verse is the hinge between argument and conspiracy.\n\n'You are only a people being tested' also reframes the whole habit of omens as part of the exam rather than part of the explanation. A test works by ambiguity: events arrive that could be read as luck, stars, or providence, and the reading a person chooses displays what is already inside him. Those who blamed a prophet were not refuting Salih; they were grading themselves, and the grade was visible to everyone but them.",
  "48": "\"And there were in the city nine elite men\" narrows the camera from the whole disbelieving party to a small, dangerous circle. The city is the settlement of Thamud. The nine are a \"raht,\" a tight band of men, described here as elite in the English rendering because they had the standing to plan a city's crime. The Quran gives their number. It does not need their personal names for the lesson to stand.\n\nWhat defines them is not their rank but their work: \"who spread corruption in the land, never doing what is right.\" Corruption, \"yufsiduna fi al-ard,\" is the opposite of setting things right. They damage the moral and social order. The second phrase is even bleaker. They do not reform. They do not mix some good with their harm. They \"never\" put things right.\n\nOther passages about Thamud mention the she-camel of Allah and the plot to hamstring her. This verse gives the human core of that corruption: a countable group who lead the land toward ruin. Great disasters in the Quran often have a small engine, a handful of people with power and no repair in their habits.\n\nThe previous verse said the people were being tested. These nine are failing the test in an active way. They are not only superstitious. They are corruptors. The next verse will put a night oath in their mouths. This verse makes sure the listener knows who is speaking: not confused farmers, but men used to spoiling the earth.\n\nThere is a warning here that does not need a modern slogan. A city can look settled while nine men inside it are already at work against every right thing. Salih's call to worship Allah was also a call away from that corruption. They heard it as a bad omen because it threatened the wrong they loved. The plot that follows is the fruit of men who would not do what is right.",
  "49": "\"They vowed, 'Let us swear by Allah'\" shows the nine corruptors dressing a murder plan in a sacred oath. They use the Name of Allah to bind one another to a crime against Allah's prophet. That is a deep twist. The tongue says Allah. The heart plans assassination.\n\nThe plan is timed: \"that we will take him and his family down by night.\" Night covers the act. Salih and his household are the target, not a battlefield of armies. The crime is meant to look like an unknown raid, not an open war.\n\nThen comes the lie prepared in advance: \"Then we will certainly say to his closest heirs, 'We did not witness the murder of his family. We are definitely telling the truth.'\" They rehearse the denial before the blood is shed. They will face the avengers or the kin and claim they never saw the killing. They will even call themselves truthful. The last sentence is shameless. False witness is built into the plot, not added later in panic.\n\nThe Quran often exposes this kind of secret planning. In other stories, people plot by night and Allah records it. Here the plot is quoted so clearly that no romance remains around it. These are not rebels with a noble cause. They are elite men who spread corruption, swearing by Allah to kill a prophet and then lie about it.\n\nA listener might wonder how anyone can say \"by Allah\" and \"we are definitely telling the truth\" in the same breath as a planned murder. The verse exists partly to show that religious words can be stolen by corrupt mouths. An oath is only as good as the heart that makes it. The next verse will answer their scheme with a sentence that has calmed many believers under plots: they made a plan, but We too made a plan, while they were unaware.",
  "50": "\"And so they made a plan\" gathers the night oath of the nine men into one word: a plan, a \"makr,\" a hidden scheme. They thought the dark would cover the murder of Salih and his family, and that a prepared lie would cover the rest. The verse does not repeat their script. It names the thing they trusted: their own plotting.\n\n\"But We too made a plan\" places Allah's planning against theirs. The same English word is used so the contrast is felt. Their plan is secret crime. Allah's plan is just decree. He is not a plotter of sin. He surrounds the plotters and brings their work to nothing. Other verses in the Quran say Allah is the best of those who plan, meaning no secret against His messengers outruns Him.\n\n\"While they were unaware\" completes the picture. The nine felt clever. They did not feel watched. Unawareness is part of their punishment already. They walk toward a trap they cannot see. The previous verse showed them rehearsing a lie for the heirs. This verse shows that the true audience of their meeting was Allah.\n\nThe story of Thamud is often told through the she-camel. This telling stresses the assassination plot. Both tellings agree that the people reached for violence against the sign of Allah and against the messenger. Planning by night does not make a people strong. It makes them blind to the larger plan.\n\nBelievers under threat have long found rest in this sentence. Enemies may meet, swear, and time a blow. None of that removes Allah's knowledge or His reply. The next verse will not leave the result vague. It will tell the listener to look at the consequences: utter destruction of them and their people together. The unaware plotters become a public lesson.\n\n'While they were unaware' also carries a grim symmetry. The nine trusted secrecy because they imagined Allah's knowledge worked like human sight, needing light and proximity. The verse quietly reminds the reader that the darkness they counted on was lit the whole time, and the unawareness they walked in was itself the first instalment of their loss, since a person who does not know he is seen cannot repent of what he is doing.",
  "51": "\"See then what the consequences of their plan were\" turns the listener into a witness. The Quran does not only narrate. It says \"see,\" as if the end of Thamud still lies open for a thinking eye. The plan of the nine is to be judged by its fruit, not by its secrecy or its oaths.\n\nThat fruit is stated without softness: \"We utterly destroyed them and their people all together.\" The nine did not fall alone. The people who shared their corruption and their refusal were taken with them. \"All together\" blocks the hope that a clever few can sin in a corner while the city stays safe. When a land's elite spread corruption and the many accept it, the blow can be shared.\n\nOther passages describe Thamud's end as a blast, a quake, or a single terrible cry. This verse does not pause on the method. It pauses on the moral result. Their plan did not kill Salih's mission. It became the road of their own destruction.\n\nA person might ask how the destruction of a whole people is just if only nine swore the oath. The chapter has already said the people split, that the disbelieving group hurried torment, that they called the prophet a bad omen, and that corruption was spread in the land. The nine were the spearpoint. They were not the only disease.\n\n\"See then\" also ties this story to Pharaoh's end earlier in the chapter, where the Quran said, \"See then what was the end of the corruptors.\" The same invitation follows two different nations. The pattern is the teaching. Plotting against a messenger is not a local incident. It is a road with a known end. The next verse will point to the empty houses that remain as a visible lesson for people of knowledge.",
  "52": "\"So their homes are there, but completely ruined\" leaves the story in the landscape. The houses of Thamud did not vanish from the earth. They stand empty, \"khawiyah,\" fallen in and vacant. Travelers in Arabia knew the carved dwellings associated with that people. The Quran points to such remains as a sermon in stone.\n\n\"Because of their wrongdoing\" gives the cause. The ruins are not a random decay of time alone. They are tied to \"zulm,\" wrongdoing, the injustice of denying Allah, harming the land, and plotting murder. Architecture cannot save a people who ruin their own souls.\n\n\"Surely in this is a lesson for people of knowledge\" names the audience who benefit. Not every passerby learns. \"People of knowledge\" are those who read events, not only walls. They connect the empty house to the filled sin. The same chapter later tells the Prophet to say, travel throughout the land and see the fate of the wicked. This verse is already that journey in one picture.\n\nA misconception would be to treat old ruins as tourism without meaning, or to invent details the Quran does not give about each doorway. The point is not a museum lecture. The point is that wrongdoing leaves a trace, and that knowledge is the ability to take warning from that trace.\n\nThe previous verse said, see the consequences. This verse shows where to look: the homes, ruined, still there. Destruction was thorough for the people, yet a sign was left for later people. Mercy even sits inside the punishment, because a lesson remains for anyone willing to know. The next verse will balance the ruin with a rescue. Not everyone in that land was left in the empty houses.\n\n'Because of their wrongdoing' also refuses the comfortable reading of ruins. Time decays all buildings, and a passerby can shrug that these simply grew old. The verse ties the emptiness to sin instead, and the tie is deliberate: decay is universal, but a civilization removed mid-pride, with its works left standing as a caution, is a message. The lesson is addressed to people of knowledge precisely because the careless will see weathering where the taught will see warning.",
  "53": "\"And We delivered those who were faithful\" is the other half of Thamud's end. Destruction was not a blind storm. Delivery, \"anjayna,\" is Allah's saving of the believers. Salih's followers, the group that did not call him a bad omen and did not join the nine, were brought out.\n\nThe verse adds a second mark: \"and were mindful of Allah.\" Faithful and mindful belong together. The word behind \"mindful\" is \"yattaqun,\" from \"taqwa,\" the watchful fear and care that keeps a person from crossing Allah's limits. They did not only say they believed. They lived on guard against the corruption around them.\n\nThis pairing appears often in the Quran. Rescue is tied to iman and taqwa, not to tribe or to being born in the same city as the doomed. Salih was their brother by blood. Brotherhood of faith is what was saved.\n\nThe queen of Sheba was delivered in another way, by guidance rather than by a blast. Thamud's believers are delivered from a blast. Different stories, same Lord who does not waste those who keep faith. The nine planned to take Salih and his family by night. Allah's plan included taking the faithful out of reach.\n\nThe verse is short because the principle is clear. After empty houses and a lesson for people of knowledge, the listener needs to know that knowledge and faith had a living party, not only a ruin. Mindfulness of Allah is not a private feeling with no result in history. In this telling, it is the difference between a destroyed people and a delivered group. The chapter now leaves Thamud and calls another messenger to mind: Lot.\n\nThe word for delivered also carries the sense of rescue through water, the same verb the Quran uses for Noah in the ark. Thamud was a dry people destroyed in a dry land, and the vocabulary still reaches for the flood image, as if deliverance, wherever it happens, shares one shape: a preserved minority carried through what buries the majority. The believers of Thamud were few enough that the Quran does not count them, and that fewness is itself part of the lesson.",
  "54": "\"And remember Lot, when he rebuked the men of his people\" opens a new story with a prophet already known from earlier chapters. Lot, nephew of Abraham in the wider Quranic account, is sent to a people famous for a sexual crime the Quran calls \"fahishah,\" a shameful deed. He does not flatter them. He rebukes them.\n\nThe rebuke is a question: \"Do you commit that shameful deed while you can see one another?\" The shame is public. They see one another in the act, or they see the wrongness with open eyes and still do it. Either reading, and both have been mentioned by commentators, stresses that this is not a hidden slip of one person. It is a practiced, visible corruption.\n\nThe Quran elsewhere details that they approached men instead of women, cut the road, and committed evil in their gatherings. This verse begins with the moral shock. A prophet must name the deed. Silence would be a kind of partnership.\n\nThe question form matters. Lot is trying to wake a conscience that still has eyes. \"While you can see\" removes the excuse of ignorance in the simple sense. They are not blind to the act. They perform it in the light of one another's sight.\n\nThis story follows Thamud so that the chapter's list of destroyed peoples stays in view for the Meccan audience who denied resurrection and mocked warning. Different sins, same pattern: a messenger speaks, a people cling to their shame, and a rain or a blast comes after the warning. The next verse will make the nature of the lust explicit, so that no one can pretend the shameful deed was unnamed.\n\n'While you can see one another' may also carry the sense of daylight openness: the deed had stopped hiding. Lot's later complaint, recorded elsewhere in the Quran, is that his people did evil even in their gatherings, and the question here presses on that publicity. A sin done in the dark can still be embarrassed; a sin that meets the eye of the group and continues has burned its last brake, and the prophet's question is aimed at whatever brake remains.",
  "55": "\"Do you really lust after men instead of women?\" continues Lot's rebuke with a second question. The first asked if they commit the shameful deed while seeing. This one names the desire: they seek men with lust, leaving the women who are the ordinary match of human pairing. The Quran treats this as a reversal of the created way, not as a small custom.\n\n\"In fact, you are only a people acting ignorantly.\" The word \"tajhaloon\" is not a claim that they lack information about their own acts. It is the ignorance of \"jahl,\" foolish heat, the refusal of wisdom, the behavior of people who throw off the limits they know. They act as if they do not know what a human community is for.\n\nLot's tone is still address, still question and verdict, not yet a prayer for stones. He is a brother-messenger trying to pull them back. The ignorance he names is moral. A people can be skilled in trade or building and still \"act ignorantly\" in the matter that ruins them.\n\nSome modern readers want this verse to be only about rape or only about hospitality. The wording is about lust after men instead of women. Other verses about Lot's people add highway robbery and public evil. Those additions do not erase this sentence. The Quran can condemn more than one crime in one people, and this verse isolates the sexual reversal as a central shame.\n\nThe next verse will show that they have no argument. Their only reply will be expulsion. When a people cannot answer a moral question, they often try to remove the questioner. Lot's questions are about to meet that political answer.\n\n'Instead of women' is stated plainly, and the plainness is part of the verse's honesty. The Quran does not bury the subject under euphemism, and it does not let the listener file the sin under vagueness. At the same time, the verdict falls on the reversal of desire and on the ignorance that surrounds it, not on the mere existence of temptation; what a people normalises and practises openly is what draws the prophetic sentence, and the sentence here is aimed at a whole city's settled conduct.",
  "56": "\"But his people's only response was to say, 'Expel Lot's followers from your land!'\" shows a people without a case. The verse says \"only response.\" They do not deny the deed. They do not explain it. They do not repent. They choose exile for the prophet's household.\n\nThe reason they give is bitter with irony: \"They are a people who wish to remain chaste!\" Purity, \"yatatahharoon,\" wanting to stay clean, is treated as a crime worth banishment. In a city that has made shame public, chastity looks like an insult. The clean make the unclean feel judged, so the unclean vote to drive the clean away.\n\nThis is one of the Quran's sharpest pictures of a reversed conscience. Good is named as the problem. The land is called \"your land,\" as if it belongs to the corrupt by right, and the chaste are guests who can be thrown out.\n\nThe same pattern appears when Mecca pressured believers to leave. A society that will not change its sin will try to change its population. Lot's followers are not accused of theft or treason. They are accused of wanting purity.\n\nThe coming verses will invert the expulsion. Allah will deliver Lot and his family, except his wife, and the land of the mockers will receive a rain of ruin. Those who said \"expel\" will themselves be removed from the earth. The wish to stay chaste, which they mocked, will be the mark of those who survive. The next verse makes the exception of the wife clear, so that kinship is not confused with salvation.\n\n'Your land' is the telling possessive. Lot had been sent to them and had lived among them for years, yet the city's property is claimed for the corrupt and denied to the chaste, and the grammar exposes the assumption underneath the eviction: whoever controls the sin controls the place. The story answers that assumption in the end by removing the claimants and preserving the prophet's name in scripture, while the land itself passed to others; ownership, the passage teaches, was never theirs to wield against purity.",
  "57": "\"So We delivered him and his family, except his wife\" follows the expulsion talk with Allah's own sorting. Delivery again, as with Salih's faithful. Lot and his family are brought out. The exception is immediate and hard: his wife is not among the saved.\n\n\"We had destined her to be one of the doomed.\" The doomed, \"al-ghabirin,\" are those left behind in the punishment. Her fate was decreed because of what she was, a woman who sided with the guilty people. Other verses say she betrayed Lot, not by the same public deed of the men, but by loyalty to the disbelievers, by pointing or informing, by remaining at heart with the city.\n\nThe verse teaches that marriage to a prophet does not save. Family is not a shield. The same chapter saved those who were faithful and mindful, not those who shared a house. Lot's wife had the closest seat to guidance and still belonged with the doomed.\n\nA misconception is to invent details of her death that the Quran does not give here, or to treat her as punished for being a woman. The text's reason is destiny tied to her being of the leftover people, the people of the crime. Another misconception is to think Lot failed as a husband. The Quran places the decree with Allah and the blame with her choice.\n\n\"So We delivered\" answers their cry to expel. They wanted the chaste out of the land. Allah takes the chaste out as rescue, and leaves the rest for the rain. The next verse is that rain. The exception of the wife makes the coming destruction morally precise. Not every person under Lot's roof was a believer. Not every person outside it needed to be named. Allah knew who was doomed.\n\n'Except his wife' also keeps the verse's precision from becoming cruelty. The Quran does not gloat and does not itemise her end; it records the exception and the reason in one clause, and elsewhere in the Book the same verdict is repeated with the same economy. Scripture here models how to speak of a doomed soul: factually, briefly, without theatre, leaving the biography of guilt to the One who read the heart.",
  "58": "\"And We poured upon them a rain of brimstone\" is the end of Lot's people. The word is \"rain,\" \"matar,\" but it is not the mercy of water that grows gardens in later verses of this chapter. It is a pouring of stones, a brimstone rain, as other passages describe baked stones marked for the criminals.\n\n\"How evil was the rain of those who had been warned!\" closes with a cry of moral disgust. They were not struck in their sleep without a messenger. They had been warned. Lot had rebuked, questioned, and named the shame. The rain is evil as a fate, not as an injustice. It is the worst rain because it falls on people who heard and then chose expulsion of the chaste over their own repentance.\n\nThe chapter earlier spoke of rain that Allah sends down so delightful gardens grow, and asked whether another god does that. Here rain is a punishment. The same sky serves mercy and judgment. Those who associate partners with Allah treat nature as a luck of idols. These two rains refuse that idea. Water and stones both obey the one Lord.\n\n\"Those who had been warned\" is a title that should haunt any later audience that hears a prophet and jokes. Warning raises responsibility. The people of Lot became a byword in the Quran for a town overturned. This verse does not linger on geography. It lingers on the quality of the rain and on the fact of prior warning.\n\nAfter Moses, Solomon, Salih, and Lot, the chapter will turn from stories to a direct debate about who is better, Allah or the partners. The stories have supplied the evidence. Peoples were warned. Some submitted, like the queen. Some were delivered, like the mindful. Some were rained upon. The next verse begins the praise and the question that those stories were meant to support.",
  "59": "\"Say, O Prophet, 'Praise be to Allah, and peace be upon the servants He has chosen.'\" After the long chain of stories, the Prophet Muhammad is told to speak praise and peace. Praise, \"al-hamd,\" belongs to Allah for His signs, His rescue of believers, and His justice against corruptors. Peace is sent upon the chosen servants, the prophets and the faithful just named: Moses, David, Solomon, Salih, Lot, and those delivered with them.\n\nThe chosen, \"istafa,\" are selected by Allah for message and for mercy. Greeting them with peace is a refusal of the Meccan habit of mocking messengers. If Allah has chosen them, the right speech is \"salam,\" not omen-talk and not expulsion.\n\nThen the verse turns: \"Ask the disbelievers, 'Which is better: Allah or whatever gods they associate with Him?'\" The question is almost too plain. After gardens of proof in the stories, can a carved partner or a worshipped sun stand beside the Lord who spoke to Moses, taught Solomon, saved Salih's faithful, and rained on Lot's town?\n\nThe Arabic challenge is \"a-Allah khayrun amma yushrikun,\" is Allah better or what they associate? The answer is built into the asking. No honest scale remains. Yet the next verses will not stop at the question. They will list creation, rain, gardens, the stable earth, rivers, mountains, the barrier between waters, the answer to the distressed, guidance in darkness, winds, origin and resurrection, and provision. The question of this verse is the heading of that whole argument.\n\nA listener in Mecca who loved ancestors' idols is being asked to compare, not merely to inherit. Comparison is a mercy. It invites the mind. The stories showed what happens when people refuse the comparison. The praise and peace at the start keep the Prophet's own heart in gratitude while he asks a question that may be met with stubborn silence.",
  "60": "\"Or ask them, 'Who created the heavens and the earth'\" begins the first detailed challenge after the general question of which is better. Creation of the heavens and the earth is the widest work. No idol of Mecca, and no sun of Sheba, laid out the sky and the ground.\n\nThe question continues, \"and sends down rain for you from the sky, by which We cause delightful gardens to grow?\" Rain is not a vague blessing. It is \"for you.\" Gardens, \"hada'iq dhata bahjah,\" are gardens with brightness and joy. The speaker shifts to \"We cause,\" so that the listener hears Allah claiming the growth, not a nature that runs alone.\n\n\"You could never cause their trees to grow\" cuts human boast. A farmer plants and waters, but he cannot create the life of the tree. The sentence is humble and exact. Human skill is real. Human creating of the tree's being is not.\n\n\"Was it another god besides Allah?\" returns the refrain that will mark this whole passage. The expected answer is no. \"Absolutely not! But they are a people who set up equals to Allah!\" The word \"ya'dilun\" means they make others equal, they set up rivals, they turn aside to counterparts. They know, in the way desert people know rain, that idols do not grow trees. Still they set up equals.\n\nThe rain of brimstone in Lot's story and this rain of gardens stand near each other on purpose. The same Lord sends both. To set up equals is to insult both the mercy of gardens and the justice of warning. The next verse will leave the garden and speak of the earth's stability, rivers, mountains, and the barrier between waters, more works that no partner performed.\n\n'For you' also aims the verse at the very people being argued with. The rain feeds their caravans' grass, their markets' grain, their families' bread, and the question makes the listeners the recipients of the evidence against their idols. An argument they could wave away as abstract becomes a matter of yesterday's supper, and supper is harder to deny than a creed.",
  "61": "\"Or ask them, 'Who made the earth a place of settlement'\" moves from sky and gardens to the ground under the feet. A \"qarar,\" a place of settlement, is earth steady enough to live on, not a lurching ruin. Human cities, including Mecca, depend on that gift before they depend on any idol.\n\n\"Caused rivers to flow through it\" adds movement of fresh water across that settled ground. \"Placed firm mountains upon it\" adds the weights and pegs that the Quran often ties to the earth's stillness. \"And set a barrier between fresh and salt bodies of water\" points to the strange meeting of seas or of river and sea, where waters remain distinct by a barrier, \"hajiz,\" that humans did not build.\n\nEach gift is a question. Who did this? \"Was it another god besides Allah?\" The refrain returns. \"Absolutely not! But most of them do not know.\" The close is different from the previous verse. There they set up equals. Here most of them lack knowledge. Both can be true. Some stubbornly equalize. Many simply do not think.\n\nThe barrier between waters is a sign that later generations have also found striking. The verse does not ask for a modern ocean lecture. It asks for honesty. The things that make settled life possible are not the work of the partners.\n\nSolomon's crystal palace and this firm earth are different kinds of making. One is a gift through a prophet's kingdom. The other is the common floor of all people. The argument is that even the common floor is already enough to end \"shirk,\" the associating of others with Allah. The next question will leave landscape and enter the human cry of distress, a proof from the heart rather than from mountains.\n\n'Most of them do not know' also grades the audience more gently than the verse before it. There, active equalizers; here, the unthinking many. The distinction matters for the Prophet's approach: some opponents need confrontation, and many need only to be helped to notice what they walk on. A religion that treats every ignorance as malice will talk past half the people it addresses.",
  "62": "\"Or ask them, 'Who responds to the distressed when they cry to Him'\" turns from mountains to the breaking voice. The \"mudtarr\" is the person in desperate need, cornered, out of tricks. When that person cries, who answers? The verse assumes a fact the pagan Arabs themselves knew in storms and illness: they would call on Allah. Other verses say that when harm touches people at sea they remember Him, then they forget when they land.\n\n\"Relieving their affliction\" is the second act. Response is not only hearing. It is the lifting of the \"su',\" the harm. Then a third act: \"and Who makes you successors in the earth.\" Humans inherit the land generation after generation, \"khulafa.\" Power to dwell and replace those before you is not self-made.\n\n\"Is it another god besides Allah?\" The refrain comes again, now in a slightly different English dress. \"Yet you are hardly mindful!\" The charge is lack of \"tadhakkur,\" reminder. They know the cry of distress in their bones and still will not keep that knowledge awake.\n\nThis verse matters because a person might admit Allah created the heavens and still take daily fears to a charm, a saint, a star, or an idol. The Quran says the One who answers the desperate is Allah. To call others in that moment as if they shared His power is the very association under attack.\n\nThe queen of Sheba was not in a sinking ship, yet she was distressed by a letter and a coming force. Her final cry was, \"My Lord! I have certainly wronged my soul.\" That is the mudtarr finding the right address. The verse asks Mecca to do the same before a rain of stones or a last Hour makes the cry too late. The next sign will be guidance in the dark of land and sea, and winds that bring mercy.",
  "63": "\"Or ask them, 'Who guides you in the darkness of the land and sea'\" speaks to a people who traveled caravans and ships. Darkness here is the dark of night and the dark of being lost, when stars, landmarks, and coasts become the difference between life and death. Who sets those signs and brings the traveler through?\n\n\"And sends the winds ushering in His mercy\" adds the winds that go ahead of rain. Mercy in this picture is often the rainfall itself, announced by moving air. Sailors and herders both knew that a wind could mean relief. The verse gives that relief a Sender.\n\n\"Is it another god besides Allah?\" The question does not grow tired. Each new gift needs the same honesty. \"Exalted is Allah above what they associate with Him!\" This close is praise, \"ta'ala Allah,\" high is Allah above the partners. The partners are not merely weaker. They are beneath His height.\n\nGuidance in darkness pairs with the chapter's opening claim that the Book is a guide for believers. Physical guidance through night and spiritual guidance through revelation come from one Lord. To take a star as a god because it helps in the dark is to confuse the sign with the One who hung the sign.\n\nThe winds \"ushering in His mercy\" also stand opposite the rain of brimstone. Winds can go before a garden or, in other stories, before a punishing storm. They are not luck. They are sent. The next verse will rise from travel and weather to the origin of creation, its resurrection, and daily provision, then demand proof from those who still claim partners.\n\nThe pairing of darkness and wind also matches the two halves of a caravan's fear: being lost, and being stranded. Guidance answers the first, the herald winds of rain answer the second, and the verse hands both anxieties to one Sender. The listener who has felt either fear recognises the argument immediately, which is why the proof is drawn from travel rather than from theory.",
  "64": "\"Or ask them, 'Who originates the creation then resurrects it'\" brings the argument to the two ends of existence. Originating, \"yabda'u al-khalq,\" is the first making. Resurrecting, \"yu'iduhu,\" is the bringing back. The pagan listener often admitted the first and denied the second. The verse ties them in one breath. The One who started is the One who returns.\n\n\"And gives you provisions from the heavens and the earth\" keeps the matter from becoming only a debate about the far future. Provision, \"rizq,\" is today's food, rain, crops, and what the soil yields. The God of resurrection is the God of supper.\n\n\"Is it another god besides Allah?\" One last time in this series the question falls. Then a new command: \"Say, O Prophet, 'Show me your proof, if what you say is true.'\" The partners now owe \"burhan,\" a clear proof. Stories, heavens, rain, gardens, earth, rivers, mountains, barriers, answers to distress, guidance, winds, origin, return, and provision have been presented. What proof do the associates have?\n\nThe demand for proof is a kindness to the mind. The Quran does not ask for blind insult of ancestors. It asks for evidence. If none exists, the honest act is to drop the partners.\n\nMeccans said resurrection was an ancient fable, as the next group of verses will quote. This verse has already answered them in principle: the Originator can resurrect, and the Provider is the same Lord. The next verse will close another door, the claim that someone besides Allah knows the unseen, including the hour of rising.\n\n'Show me your proof' also sets a rule that outlives the scene. The demand for evidence runs one way through this passage: creation is presented as the believers' proof, and the associates are asked for theirs. Ever since, the Quran's method has invited the same test, and the honest answer it predicts is silence, because a partner that cannot grow a tree cannot produce a witness either.",
  "65": "\"Say, O Prophet, 'None in the heavens and the earth has knowledge of the unseen except Allah.'\" After demanding proof for partners, the Quran shuts the claim of hidden knowledge. The unseen, \"al-ghayb,\" is what lies beyond created sight: the future, the Hour, the secrets of the womb of time. Angels, jinn, humans, and the beings of the heavens do not own it. Allah alone knows it.\n\nSolomon's story in this chapter could be misread. A hoopoe brought news. A man of Scripture brought a throne in a blink. Jinn spoke of strength. None of that means creatures possess the unseen as Allah does. They know what they are taught or what they observe. This verse protects tawhid, the oneness of Allah, in the field of knowledge.\n\n\"Nor do they know when they will be resurrected.\" The most urgent unseen for the deniers is the time of rising. They ask \"when\" as if the messenger were hiding a date, or as if not knowing a date made the event false. The verse says no one in the heavens or the earth knows that when. Not knowing the hour is not ignorance of Allah. It is the created condition.\n\nA common mistake is to go to fortune-tellers, star-readers, or anyone who claims the ghayb. This verse leaves no room for that trade. Another mistake is to think the Prophet failed because he did not give a calendar of the Hour. He is commanded to say that none knows except Allah.\n\nThe next verse will describe the deniers' so-called knowledge of the Hereafter as ignorance, doubt, and blindness. This verse has prepared that charge. If they do not know the unseen, their confident mockery of resurrection is empty. They speak without the only knowledge that would settle the \"when.\" \n\n'Nor do they know when they will be resurrected' also removes a lever the mockers thought they held. Their strongest card was the messenger's lack of a date, and the verse simply concedes the point and re-sits it: no creature holds that card, so its absence proves nothing against the message. An argument built on a gap in created knowledge collapses once the gap is acknowledged as designed.",
  "66": "\"No! Their knowledge of the Hereafter amounts to ignorance.\" The English line captures a dense Arabic turn: their knowledge about the last day has not matured; it has collapsed into not-knowing. They talk as if they have researched the matter and found it false. The Quran says the result of their \"knowledge\" is ignorance.\n\n\"In fact, they are in doubt about it.\" The first step down is ignorance. The second is \"shakk,\" doubt. They are not calmly sure there is no rising. They waver, yet they mock, which is a strange combination. Mockery often hides a shaken heart.\n\n\"In truth, they are totally blind to it.\" The third step is \"amun,\" blindness. Doubt that is not treated becomes a refusal to see. The Hereafter is not dim because it lacks signs. It is dim because they have closed the eye that should use the signs just listed: origin and return, rain and ruin, delivered believers and empty houses.\n\nThe verse uses a climbing correction: no, in fact, in truth. Each clause is stronger. A teacher might stop at \"they do not know.\" The Quran says they do not know, they doubt, they are blind. That is why argument alone sometimes fails, as later verses will tell the Prophet he cannot make the dead hear.\n\nThis diagnosis also guards believers from envy of the deniers' confidence. That confidence is not knowledge. It is blindness wearing the mask of sophistication. The next verses will quote their actual sentences about dust and ancient fables, so the listener can hear the blindness in their own words.\n\nThe three-step descent also describes how the same soul can hold doubt and mockery at once. Ignorance is passive, doubt is unsettled, blindness is chosen; the mocker has travelled the full distance and speaks from the end of it. Believers meet the resulting confidence and mistake it for strength, when the verse has already diagnosed it as the final stage of not-seeing.",
  "67": "\"The disbelievers ask, 'When we and our fathers are reduced to dust, will we really be brought forth alive?'\" Now the blindness speaks. The question is built on a picture: bodies become dust, fathers already dust, so how can bringing forth happen? The appeal is to what the eye sees in graves.\n\nThe wording \"we and our fathers\" tries to make the case heavier. Not only we will be powder. The old generations are already powder. If anyone were coming back, the fathers would have come. The argument sounds practical. It treats decay as a disproof of power.\n\nThe Quran has just said that none knows the unseen except Allah, and that the Originator can resurrect. This question does not answer those points. It only stares at dust. Dust is real. The limitation of dust over Allah is not real.\n\nSimilar questions appear in many chapters. The repetition means the doubt was a standard line in Mecca, not a rare thought. \"Brought forth alive\" is said with disbelief, not with hope. The next verse will add that they have heard this promise before and have filed it under ancient fables.\n\nLot's people and Thamud also refused a world beyond their custom. Dust-talk is another custom of refusal. The body they cannot imagine being remade is the same body that eats provision from the heavens and the earth, provision they cannot create. The question would be honest if it ended in humility: how will Allah do this? It is not honest when it ends in a sneer. The following verse makes the sneer plain.\n\n'Reduced to dust' also borrows its force from a half-truth, which is why the question survives generations. Bodies really do decay; the error is the hidden premise that decay limits the Maker. The Quran's replies never dispute the dust; they dispute the premise, and the same reasoning applies wherever a true observation is made to carry a false conclusion.",
  "68": "\"We have already been promised this, as well as our forefathers earlier.\" The disbelievers claim boredom. The promise of rising is not new to them. Their fathers heard it. They hear it. Nothing has happened yet, so they treat delay as cancellation.\n\n\"This is nothing but ancient fables!\" is the stamp they put on revelation. \"Asatir al-awwalin,\" tales of the ancients, was a stock insult in Mecca against the Quran. They filed resurrection with old stories told to children, not with truth that binds adults.\n\nThe insult tries to reverse honor. What is old can be venerable, like the message of Noah and Abraham. They use oldness as a slur. Because Salih and Lot and Moses already warned, the Meccans say the warning is a recycled myth. The chapter's stories were meant to show the opposite: the warning is old because the truth is one.\n\nDelay of the Hour was explained in the verse that none knows when they will be resurrected. They turn that hidden timing into proof that the promise is a fable. Hidden timing is a test. They fail it by mockery.\n\nThe next command will not be a long philosophy of dust. It will be, travel the land and see the fate of the wicked. Empty homes of Thamud and overturned towns of Lot are not fables in the sand. They are remains. If the old warnings were only stories, why do the ruins match them? The Quran sends the mocker from the word \"fable\" to the road.\n\n'As well as our forefathers earlier' also reveals how tradition cuts both ways in their hands. Ancestry is cited as authority against the promise, though the promise is older than the ancestry: Noah warned, and so did those after him. What the mockers inherit is not the original hearing of the message but the accumulated habit of ignoring it, and they mistake the pile of years for evidence.",
  "69": "\"Say, O Prophet, 'Travel throughout the land and see the fate of the wicked.'\" The reply to \"ancient fables\" is a journey. \"Siru fi al-ard,\" travel in the earth. Look. The fate, \"aqibah,\" of the criminals is not only written. It is stamped on places.\n\n\"The wicked\" here are \"al-mujrimin,\" those who cut themselves off by crime and denial. The chapter has already walked the listener past Pharaoh's end, Thamud's ruined homes, and the evil rain of Lot's people. The Prophet is to send his people to such traces, or to any land where a similar end can still be read.\n\nTravel in the Quran is not always trade. Sometimes it is a school. A person who will not accept unseen resurrection may still accept a seen ruin. The ruin does not itself raise the dead. It shows that Allah's warning in this world is not a fable. The God who did that can do the bringing forth they mock.\n\n\"See\" repeats the earlier \"see then what the consequences of their plan were.\" The same eye is invited again. Knowledge in this chapter is often the knowledge that looks.\n\nThe verse does not tell the Prophet to invent new threats. It tells him to point at the earth. That is a calm answer to sarcasm. The following verse will care for the Prophet's own heart: do not grieve for them, and do not be distressed by their schemes. After commanding a public reply, Allah will command an inner rest. The travel is for the mockers. The comfort is for the messenger who has to listen to them call truth a fable.\n\n'Travel' also dignifies the physical act of going and looking. The Quran does not ask the skeptic to feel something or to trust the messenger's face; it asks him to walk to a site and read it. Faith in this Book is repeatedly invited through eyes, feet, and ruins, which leaves the stay-at-home mocker without even the excuse of distance.",
  "70": "\"Do not grieve for them\" is spoken to the Prophet after he is told to send people traveling to see the fate of the wicked. The command turns from the public answer to the messenger's heart. Grief here is the sorrow of a man who wants his people saved and watches them choose mockery instead. Other verses tell him not to nearly destroy himself with grief over their denial. This line belongs to that gentle series.\n\n\"Nor be distressed by their schemes\" names the second weight on him. They do not only disbelieve. They plot, as Thamud's nine plotted, as Mecca plotted against the Prophet. Distress, tightness in the chest, is a natural human response to schemes. Allah forbids that tightness from ruling him. Their \"makr\" is seen. It is not allowed to shrink his mission.\n\nThe two halves treat two different pains. Grief looks backward and inward at their loss. Distress looks at their active harm. Both are closed as masters of the Prophet's soul. He may feel, but he may not sink.\n\nThis is not coldness. A prophet who did not care would not need the command. The command exists because he cares too much for his own peace. Allah takes the burden of results. The Prophet carries the message. The travel-command of the previous verse is for them. This rest-command is for him.\n\nSchemes in this chapter have already been answered in the story of Salih: they made a plan, but We too made a plan, while they were unaware. The Prophet is to live that truth, not only recite it. When the next verse quotes them asking when the threat will come, he will answer without the panic of a man undone by grief. Inner rest is part of clear speech.\n\nThe pairing also matches the Prophet's two job hazards exactly: sorrow for those who will not be saved, and fear of those who scheme. One is answered with comfort, the other with the reminder that scheming was already answered in this chapter, against the nine. A heart kept free of both is not a heart without love or caution; it is a heart whose love and caution have both been handed their proper size.",
  "71": "\"They ask the believers, 'When will this threat come to pass, if what you say is true?'\" The mockery now takes the form of a calendar question. \"This threat\" is the promised punishment or the Hour they were told to fear. They do not ask in order to prepare. They ask in order to imply that delay means falsehood.\n\n\"If what you say is true\" puts the believers in the dock. The burden of proof, in their mouths, is a date. The previous verses already said that none in the heavens or the earth knows the unseen except Allah, and that they do not know when they will be resurrected. This question pretends that a true prophet must still produce a schedule.\n\nThe same spirit appeared when Salih asked why they hastened the torment rather than grace. Haste for punishment is not piety. It is a dare. Mecca dares the warning the way Thamud dared Salih.\n\nThe believers are the ones being asked, not only the Prophet. Everyday companions faced this taunt in markets and gatherings. The next verse will give the Prophet a sentence to say, so the community is not left fumbling for a clever date they were never given.\n\nA misconception is to treat every \"when\" as honest curiosity. Honest curiosity seeks how to be ready. This \"when\" seeks how to laugh. The wording \"if what you say is true\" already leans toward the charge of lying. The Quran will not satisfy that lean with a number. It will answer with nearness as a possibility, with bounty they already waste, and with the Lord's knowledge of what they hide.\n\n'They ask the believers' also shows the taunt working at street level. The question was not reserved for formal debates with the Prophet; ordinary Muslims met it in the market, and the verse records the plural askers and the plural asked. Scripture here acknowledges the social pressure of being laughed at, and the reply it supplies is one any companion could carry and reuse.",
  "72": "\"Say, O Prophet, 'Perhaps some of what you seek to hasten is close at hand.'\" The answer refuses a date and refuses a shrug. \"Perhaps\" keeps the unseen with Allah. \"Close at hand\" removes the comfort of thinking the threat is always far. Some of what they hurry toward may already be near: a worldly blow, a defeat, a death, or the first shadows of the Hour. The verse does not specify which piece. Nearness is enough to end careless delay.\n\nThey \"seek to hasten.\" The verb puts the haste on them, as Salih put it on his people. The Prophet does not invent a new punishment to match their dare. He warns that the very thing they drag forward may be at the door.\n\nEarly Muslims later saw parts of this nearness in the struggles of the prophetic years, but the wording stays broader than one battle. Any listener who asks \"when\" as a joke is told that joking does not push the matter into a safe century.\n\nThe sentence is short so it can be remembered and repeated. It matches the command not to grieve. A grieving messenger might over-speak, or fall silent. This line is measured. It neither names a day nor lets them sleep.\n\n\"Some of what you seek\" also hints that not all promised things arrive at once. Resurrection of all dust is one thing. A nearer taste of consequence may be another. The mocker who only watches the sky for the final blast may miss the nearer justice already walking. The next verse will explain why they are given time at all: the Lord is bountiful, and most of them meet that bounty with ingratitude.\n\n'Perhaps' also teaches the Prophet a rhetoric of restraint. He is not licensed to threaten a specific day, and he is not permitted to dismiss the danger either; the word chosen leaves the timetable with Allah while unsettling the mocker's ease. Believers have long noted that this single sentence models honest speech under provocation: definite about the reality, indefinite about the date, serious about both.",
  "73": "\"Surely your Lord is ever Bountiful to humanity\" explains the delay they misuse. The Lord of the Prophet is \"dhu fadl,\" possessor of bounty, toward people as people, not only toward believers. Rain, night for rest, day for sight, provision, and the holding back of sudden ruin are all fadl. If every dare were answered at once with stones, the world would not be a place of trial.\n\n\"But most of them are ungrateful.\" The word \"la yashkurun\" returns the theme Solomon spoke beside the throne: whoever is grateful, it is for his own good; whoever is ungrateful, the Lord is Self-Sufficient, Most Generous. Most people eat the bounty and do not thank. Delay, which is itself a bounty, becomes fuel for the question \"when.\"\n\nThe verse balances two facts that cynics separate. They say, if God were real and angry, He would strike now. The Quran says, He is real and bountiful, and the strike's delay is part of that bounty, and most waste it. Ingratitude is not only failing to say thanks. It is using extra time to mock the warning.\n\n\"Your Lord\" comforts the Prophet. The Lord who told him not to grieve is the same Lord whose bounty fills the mockers' markets. Their safety today is not proof against him. It is proof of Allah's fadl.\n\nSolomon asked to be inspired to thanks. This verse says most humans take the opposite road. The chapter keeps setting gratitude and ingratitude opposite each other, from the ant's valley to this comment on humanity. The next verse will add that the Lord knows what they hide and what they show, so the ungrateful heart is not a secret.\n\n'To humanity' also keeps the bounty unearned and general, and the point is structural: rain does not check beliefs before falling. A mercy gated by gratitude would not be mercy but wages, and the Quran insists on the difference. The question the mockers should be asking is not why punishment waits, but why breakfast arrived again this morning to people who used yesterday's to sneer.",
  "74": "\"And surely your Lord knows what their hearts conceal and what they reveal.\" Bounty might look, to a shallow eye, like absence of knowledge, as if Allah did not see the hidden sneer. This verse closes that thought. The Lord knows the concealed, \"ma tukinnu suduruhum,\" what the chests fold away, and He knows the revealed, what they announce.\n\nThe pair matches an earlier line in the hoopoe's speech: Allah knows what you conceal and what you reveal. The same Lord of the Mighty Throne is the Lord of this verse. Knowledge of secrets is not a new claim. It is applied now to the mockers of the Hour.\n\nConcealed things include doubt they will not admit, envy, plots, and the part of the heart that fears the warning while the mouth laughs. Revealed things include the question \"when,\" the charge of ancient fables, and the schemes that distress the Prophet. Both layers are seen.\n\nThis knowledge makes delay more serious, not less. If Allah did not know the hidden, delay might be neglect. Because He knows, delay is a known test. Ingratitude is recorded from the inside out.\n\nFor the Prophet, the verse is also relief. He does not need to uncover every chest. He cannot. The Lord who forbade his grief already reads those chests. The next verse widens the same truth from their hearts to every hidden thing in heaven and earth, written in a perfect Record. Personal knowledge becomes cosmic record-keeping, so no one imagines that only loud sins are filed.\n\nConcealment, in the Quran's picture, is also never complete even from the person concealing. A mocker can hide the fear from his friends and partly from himself, and the verse calmly states that a third Reader was never blocked. That is why knowledge of hearts, far from being a threat only, can be a relief: someone always knew the whole of it, including the parts that never got spoken.",
  "75": "\"For there is nothing hidden in the heavens or the earth without being written in a perfect Record.\" The reason the Lord knows chests is larger than those chests. No hidden thing, \"gha'ibah,\" in sky or ground escapes writing. The Record, \"kitab mubin,\" is a clear, perfect book. The chapter began by calling the Quran a clear Book. Here another \"clear book\" is the register of all that is concealed in creation.\n\nCommentators have understood this as the Preserved Tablet, or as Allah's complete knowledge spoken of as writing so that humans grasp that nothing is forgotten. The point for the mocker is simple. The unseen they deny is already written. The date they demand is known to Allah even if it is unknown to them. The scheme they whisper is not off the page.\n\n\"In the heavens or the earth\" leaves no third place to hide. Jinn in the lower heaven, secrets in houses, plots in a city of nine men, the queen's old sun-worship, the wife's inner loyalty to Lot's people—all sit inside this claim.\n\nA person might fear that such a Record is only a threat. It is also justice. Good that no one clapped for is written. Hidden patience is written. The next verses will praise the Quran as a clarifier and a mercy. The Record of hidden things and the Quran that is recited work together: one preserves reality, the other explains guidance. Neither is a fable of the ancients. Both are \"mubin,\" clear.\n\nThis verse also answers fortune-tellers from another side. They do not have the Record. Claiming the unseen is claiming what is only with Allah and His writing. The Prophet is told to say none knows the ghayb except Allah. This Record is why that sentence is true.\n\n'Written' also settles the matter of memory. Human records lose files, and human witnesses forget; writing in the Quran's idiom is the guarantee that nothing true is ever mislaid. The mockers' hidden words, the believers' hidden patience, the queen's hidden deliberation, all are kept with a keeper's precision, and the Day of Judgment is run from records that cannot decay.",
  "76": "\"Indeed, this Quran clarifies for the Children of Israel most of what they differ over.\" The Book that was called a guide at the opening is now given a special work among the Jews of the Prophet's time and their disputes. They differed over many matters of law, stories of prophets, and claims about Jesus and about their own scriptures. The Quran comes as a judge and explainer of most of those differences, \"yaqussu,\" it relates and makes clear.\n\n\"Most\" is an honest word. Not every minor historical quarrel is the Quran's subject. The great differences that affect faith and practice are brought into the light. Stories in this very chapter, Moses at the fire, David and Solomon, are part of that clarifying. Israel knew those names. The Quran retells them without the clutter of later argument, and with a single message: worship Allah.\n\nThis is not said to insult the Children of Israel. It is said to honor the Quran's role and to invite them. A book that settles differences is a mercy if people accept the settlement. If they do not, the next verses will say the Lord will judge among them.\n\nMeccan pagans called the Quran ancient fables. This verse says it is a clarification of live disputes among a people of earlier scripture. Fables do not settle scholarly and religious differences. A clear Book does.\n\nThe hoopoe said he came from Sheba with sure news. The Quran comes to Israel with sure news about what they contest. Sure news demands a response like Solomon's: we will see whether you are telling the truth, then a fair test, not a sneer. The following verse will name the Quran again as guide and mercy, but this time for the believers who take it as such.\n\n'Most' also protects the Book from being reduced to a dispute-settler. The Quran's main work is guidance and mercy, and it clarifies controversies as they serve that work, not as a catalogue of every quarrel a scholar might raise. The restraint is a signature: revelation answers what matters for souls, and leaves the rest to the fields it never claimed.",
  "77": "\"And it is truly a guide and mercy for the believers.\" The opening of the chapter said the Book is a guide and good news for the believers who pray, give, and are sure of the Hereafter. This later verse returns to that description with \"mercy\" in place of \"good news,\" or rather as its sister. Guidance shows the road. Mercy is the kindness of being shown it and of being saved by walking it.\n\n\"For the believers\" is the same limit as before. The Quran is itself guidance, but it becomes a lived guide and mercy for those who believe. Others can hear the same recitation and call it a fable. The difference is not that the Book changed. The receiving heart changed the result.\n\nAfter saying the Quran clarifies differences for the Children of Israel, this verse states the inner fruit for anyone who believes, Israelite or not. Clarification can remain an outside argument. Mercy enters the life.\n\nThe stories of this chapter are part of that mercy. A believer learns how to thank like Solomon, how to consult like the queen at her best, how not to plot like the nine, how not to expel the chaste like Lot's people, how to ask who created the gardens. Mercy is practical. It is not only a soft feeling.\n\nThe next verse will speak of the Lord judging between them with justice. Guide and mercy do not cancel judgment. They make the people who refuse the guide without excuse, and they make the people who accept it hopeful. The pair of verses, clarification and mercy, then judgment, is a complete picture of what a revealed Book does in the world.\n\n'For the believers' also makes the chapter's own audience the exhibit. Everything the surah has narrated, from Moses' fire to the ant's valley, functions as guidance precisely where faith receives it, and the same recitation passing an unbelieving ear settles nothing there. The Book does not adjust itself; hearts do, and the verse quietly makes reception the hinge of the whole difference.",
  "78": "\"Your Lord will certainly judge between them by His justice.\" The differences of the Children of Israel, and the disputes of all who argue over the Quran, do not hang forever. The Lord will judge, \"yaqdi,\" with His judgment. \"By His justice\" means the verdict is not tribal and not random. \"Hukm\" here is wise ruling.\n\n\"For He is the Almighty, All-Knowing.\" The two names \"al-Aziz\" and \"al-Alim\" guarantee the judgment. Might means no party can block the sentence. Knowledge means no file is missing. Together they answer the fear that history is only the victory of the loud.\n\nMoses heard \"the Almighty, All-Wise\" at the fire. Here the pair is Almighty and All-Knowing. Wisdom and knowledge both sit with might. The names fit the task of judging differences. A weak judge cannot enforce. An ignorant judge cannot be fair.\n\n\"Between them\" can include Israel, the believers, and the pagans. All the files of this chapter wait for that court: Pharaoh who knew the signs and denied, the queen who submitted, Thamud who planned, Lot's people who expelled, the mockers who asked when.\n\nThe Prophet is not told to finish every argument in the street. He is told that the Lord will judge. That sentence works with \"do not grieve\" and with \"put your trust\" in the next verse. Human debate has a limit. Divine justice does not. The Quran clarifies most of what they differ over. What people still refuse will not remain an open tie. The Almighty, All-Knowing will close it.\n\n'Between them' also spares the Prophet a role he was never given. Judge among scripture's disputants is above his pay and above his lifespan; messenger is his office, and the verse hands the verdict upward. Every believer who argues religion has the same boundary to learn: present, clarify, invite, and then leave the closing of the case to the Almighty, All-Knowing.",
  "79": "\"So put your trust in Allah\" is the practical fruit of knowing that the Lord will judge with might and knowledge. Trust, \"tawakkul,\" is to place the weight of the affair on Allah while still doing the work of reciting and warning. It is not sitting down. It is refusing to let results become a second god.\n\n\"For you are surely upon the Path of clear truth.\" The reason he can trust is not a guess about tomorrow. It is a fact about today. He stands on \"al-haqq al-mubin,\" the clear truth. The chapter's first verse called the Book \"mubin.\" The Prophet's path is that same clarity. He does not need the crowd's agreement to know where he stands.\n\nThis address is intimate. \"You\" is the messenger whose grief and distress were forbidden, whose mouth was given answers about nearness and proof and the unseen. Now his inner act is named: trust. The path is already clear. Trust keeps him on it when schemes and taunts try to pull him into their weather.\n\nCompanions learned tawakkul from this kind of verse as well as from the Prophet's life in poverty, travel, and battle. They worked, then left the harvest to Allah. A misconception is that trust means no planning. Solomon planned a letter, a test of the throne, a disguised seat. He also said, this is by the grace of my Lord to test me. That mix is tawakkul.\n\nThe next two verses will limit the Prophet from the other side. He cannot make the dead hear. Trust does not mean he will convert everyone. Trust means he remains on the clear truth whether they hear or turn their backs.\n\n'Clear truth' also anchors trust in something checkable rather than in mood. The Prophet is not told to trust because things will go well; he is told to trust because he stands on truth that can be seen to be clear, in a Book whose arguments this chapter has run from gardens to ruins. Trust with that foundation survives bad weeks, which is precisely what it is for.",
  "80": "\"You certainly cannot make the dead hear the truth.\" The dead here are first a picture of those whose hearts have died, though the wording also reminds of literal corpses. The Prophet's voice cannot force life into a heart Allah has not opened. Other verses say the dead will hear a cry on the Day of Rising. That hearing is Allah's act, not the messenger's.\n\n\"Nor can you make the deaf hear the call when they turn their backs and walk away.\" A second picture: the deaf who also turn and leave. Two barriers, inner and outer. They do not hear, and they increase the distance. The call, \"al-du'a,\" is the invitation of the Quran. The Prophet is not blamed when such people walk off.\n\nThese images follow \"you are upon the clear truth\" so that he does not measure the truth by the number of listeners who stay. Clear truth can be spoken to backs. That does not stain the truth.\n\nThe stories already showed this limit. Moses came with enlightening signs. Hearts were convinced and still denied. Salih urged forgiveness. They spoke of omens. Lot rebuked. They voted expulsion. The Prophet of Mecca is in that line. Inability to force hearing is part of being a human messenger.\n\nA misconception is to stop inviting because some are \"dead.\" The verse limits power, not duty. He still says, still recites. He simply does not own the result. The next verse will add the blind and then state the exception: those who believe in the revelations and submit. The negative pictures prepare a positive remnant.",
  "81": "\"Nor can you lead the blind out of their misguidance.\" A third picture joins the dead and the deaf: the blind. Misguidance, \"dalalah,\" is a lost road. The Prophet cannot take a person by the hand out of that lostness if the person will not see. Leading here is \"hadi,\" the kind of guiding that actually extracts someone from error. Ultimate extraction belongs to Allah.\n\n\"You can make none hear the truth except those who believe in Our revelations, fully submitting to Allah.\" After three impossibilities comes the one possibility. Hearing happens for those who believe in the signs and are \"muslimun,\" submitting. The same word closed Solomon's letter and the queen's confession. Submission is the hearing ear.\n\nThe verse does not flatter the Prophet with half a power. It gives him a precise field. His recitation reaches everyone physically. The hearing that changes a life belongs to believers who yield. That is why the Book is a guide and mercy \"for the believers\" a few verses ago.\n\nThe queen was not forced. She reasoned, tested, saw, and said she had wronged her soul. That is the path of those who come to hear. Pharaoh's people saw and called it magic. That is the path of the blind who remain blind.\n\nFor a caller in any age, the sentence prevents two errors: despair, because some will hear; and pride, because he did not pull the blind out by his own rope. The next verse will leap from this human limit to a sign of the Hour, a beast from the earth that will speak to people about their lack of sure faith. When human calling is refused long enough, another kind of address will come.",
  "82": "\"And when the decree of the Hour comes to pass against them\" moves from the Prophet's limited calling to a future sign. The \"qawl,\" the word or decree, falling upon them means the matter of the end is due, the time of a great sign has arrived. It is not the Prophet's schedule. It is Allah's.\n\n\"We will bring forth for them a beast from the earth.\" The \"dabba\" is a creature, a beast, coming out of the earth. Authentic reports from the Prophet mention a beast among the major signs of the Hour, speaking to people. The Quran here states the fact and the message. It does not describe the beast's shape, and later storytellers have added colors and forms that the Book does not require. What is required is to believe the sign as stated.\n\nThe beast will be \"telling them that the people had no sure faith in Our revelations.\" The message is a verdict on \"yaqin,\" sure faith. This chapter began by praising those who have sure faith in the Hereafter, and it blamed those who do not believe in it. The beast's speech returns to that opening. People lived without certainty in the signs. The creature of the earth will say so.\n\nThere is a striking reversal. Humans refused to hear a human prophet. A beast will speak, and the decree will already have come to pass. Hearing at that point is not the hearing of repentance that changes a nation's road in the usual way. It is a sign that the Hour's word has fallen.\n\nA misconception is to spend this verse on speculation and pictures. The verse's own center is the charge: no sure faith in the revelations. That is a charge a person can still answer today by taking the Quran as guide and mercy. The next verses leave the beast and show the gathering of deniers in ranks, the questioning, and the silence of those against whom the decree is justified.",
  "83": "\"Watch for the Day We will gather from every faith-community a group of those who denied Our revelations.\" After the beast comes a scene of gathering, \"nahshur.\" From every \"ummah,\" every community that had a message, a troop of deniers is collected. Denial of revelations is the shared crime across nations. Pharaoh's people, Thamud, Lot's town, and Mecca's mockers all fit the description.\n\n\"And they will be driven in ranks.\" The word \"yooza'oon\" was used earlier when Solomon's forces were perfectly organized. Here the same kind of ordering is applied to prisoners of the Day, held in rows, not allowed to scatter. The echo is bitter. Solomon's ranks were a gift of kingdom. These ranks are a herding to judgment.\n\n\"A group\" from every community does not mean only a few deniers existed. It pictures them brought as companies, representatives of a type, or waves of the larger mass of rejecters. The point is the worldwide, history-wide nature of the court. No community that denied is missing from the summons.\n\nThe Prophet who could not make the dead hear will see, on that Day, that hearing is no longer the issue. Gathering is the issue. The revelations they denied will be the file in front of them.\n\nThis verse should not be read as a reason to despair of whole nations now. It is a reason to take denial seriously. Communities have an end, and deniers within them have a rank in a march they cannot refuse. The next verse will put Allah's question in their faces: did you deny without even comprehending, or what exactly did you do?",
  "84": "\"When they finally come before their Lord, He will ask them, 'Did you deny My revelations without even comprehending them?'\" The ranks arrive. The first question attacks a common method of denial: rejecting what one has not understood. \"Without even comprehending\" means they did not surround the signs with knowledge. They judged before study, mocked before thought, called the Quran a fable before they let it clarify anything.\n\n\"Or what exactly did you do?\" is the second fork. If they claim they did understand, then what was their work? Understanding plus denial is Pharaoh's crime in this chapter: hearts convinced, tongues arrogant. Understanding plus obedience is the believers' road. The question leaves them no third shelter. Either they denied in ignorance they chose, or they denied after knowing. Both are blameworthy in different ways.\n\nThe Lord says \"My revelations.\" The signs were His, not the Prophet's private verses. Denial was personal toward Allah.\n\nThis questioning matches the demand earlier: show me your proof if you are true. On that Day the proof they never had will be asked for again, when showing it is too late. The queen asked for time and sent a gift to learn. These companies refused to learn.\n\nThe verse is terrible in its calm. There is no shouting in the English line. There is a precise cross-examination. The next verse will say the decree of torment is justified and they are left speechless. The questions of this verse are why the mouth closes. When both ignorance and action have been exposed, speech has nowhere to stand.",
  "85": "\"And the decree of torment will be justified against them for their wrongdoing.\" The \"qawl\" falls again, now as the justified sentence of punishment. \"Justified\" means it comes due because of \"zulm,\" their wrongdoing, not because of a random wrath. The questions have been asked. The file of denial without comprehension, or denial after knowledge, is complete. The word against them is deserved.\n\n\"Leaving them speechless.\" They \"la yantiqoon,\" they do not speak. Earlier they had many words: when will it come, ancient fables, you are a bad omen, expel the chaste, this is magic. The court takes the gift of speech away, or the terror and the proof take it away. There is nothing left that can help.\n\nSpeechless is the opposite of the beast that spoke and of the Quran that clarifies. Those who would not use the tongue for truth lose the tongue for defense. Other verses say their hands and feet will testify. This verse only needs their silence.\n\nThe Prophet was told he cannot make them hear. On this Day they cannot make themselves speak. The two limits frame a life of warning in between, which is the only time talk can still change an end.\n\nWrongdoing here gathers the chapter's crimes: associating partners, mocking resurrection, plotting murder, public shame, arrogant denial of signs. The decree is not a thin technicality. It is the moral sum. The next verse will suddenly point back to an everyday sign they can still see before that silence: night for rest and a bright day. Mercy still teaches while speech remains.",
  "86": "\"Do they not see that We made the night for them to rest in and the day bright?\" Before the Trumpet of the next verse, the Quran pulls the eye to a sign that happens every twenty-four hours. Night is made \"to rest in,\" a mercy of stillness. Day is \"mubsiran,\" bright, making sight possible. The two are fashioned, not accidental.\n\n\"For them\" makes the gift personal. The same people who ask when the threat will come sleep under a night they did not invent and work under a day they cannot switch on.\n\n\"Surely in this are signs for those who believe.\" The signs are there for all eyes, but they function as signs for believers, as the Book functions as a guide for believers. Faith reads the pair of night and day as designed mercy. Denial sleeps and wakes as if owed the cycle.\n\nThe word \"see\" continues the chapter's habit of inviting sight: see the end of corruptors, see the ruined homes, travel and see, do they not see. Physical seeing of night and day should help the heart see the Maker.\n\nThis verse also softens the horror around it. A God who designs rest is not a God who only threatens. The people who will be speechless later were given nights of sleep in which they could have turned in repentance. Ingratitude, already named, includes treating rest as a right and dawn as a routine without praise.\n\nSolomon asked to do good that pleases his Lord. A believer can treat sleep and waking as a small daily submission. The next verse will show a Day when rest is gone and horror seizes the heavens and the earth at the Trumpet, except whom Allah wills to spare.",
  "87": "\"And beware of the Day the Trumpet will be blown\" sets a sound against the quiet of the night of rest. The \"sur,\" the Trumpet, is the signal of the Hour in many verses. When it is blown, ordinary cycles of sleep and work end.\n\n\"And all those in the heavens and all those on the earth will be horrified to the point of death, except those Allah wills to spare.\" The terror, \"faza',\" is universal among creatures. Angels, jinn, humans, the populations of sky and ground, are seized by it. The exception is real but unnamed here. Allah's will spares whom He wills. Commentators have mentioned angels nearest the Throne, or martyrs, or others. The text leaves the exception with Allah, which is safer than a list the Book did not lock.\n\n\"And all will come before Him, fully humbled.\" After horror comes attendance. \"Dakhirin\" means low, small, brought down. The arrogance of Pharaoh, the boasts of Sheba's chiefs about military might, the nine men's oath, the laugh about fables, all arrive as humility. They come; they are not invited as guests. They attend as subjects.\n\nThe verse gathers the whole cosmos, not one town. Earlier destructions were local rains and blasts. This Day is not local. That is why dust of fathers is no obstacle. The Trumpet that horrifies the heavens can raise the dust.\n\nFor the believer, the exception and the later promise of security from horror in the next verses become hope. For the denier, the picture answers \"when\" better than a date: when the Trumpet blows, dates will not matter. The next verse will add a visual shock: mountains the eye thought fixed will travel like clouds under the design of the One who perfected everything.",
  "88": "\"Now you see the mountains, thinking they are firmly fixed, but they are travelling just like clouds.\" The eye's habit is to treat mountains as the definition of stillness. The verse says that on that Day, or in the deeper reality Allah names, they move as clouds move. Clouds look slow and large and still from far away, yet they pass. Mountains will pass like that. The \"jamidah,\" the seeming solid, is not the last word.\n\nSome have also heard in this a hint that even now mountains are not the absolute fixtures people imagine. The verse's home, though, is the Day whose Trumpet was just blown, when the earth is rearranged. Other passages say mountains will be like plucked wool or scattered dust. Travelling like clouds belongs to that family of images.\n\n\"That is the design of Allah, Who has perfected everything.\" The movement is not chaos winning. It is \"sun' Allah,\" Allah's workmanship, the same perfection that made night for rest and day bright. He who perfected the still mountain can perfect its motion. \"Atqana\" means He made everything firm and well.\n\n\"Surely He is All-Aware of what you do.\" The One who handles mountains like clouds is not unaware of human deeds. The jump from geology to morality is intended. If your landmark can travel, your hidden act is not too small to be known.\n\nA person who asked how dust can rise is shown a greater wonder: the pegs of the earth on the move. The argument is by increase. The next verse will tell the frightened viewer what happens to the one who comes with a good deed: something better as reward, and security from the very horror the Trumpet caused.",
  "89": "\"Whoever comes with a good deed will be rewarded with what is better.\" On the Day of horror and traveling mountains, the file is not only crimes. A person who comes with \"hasanah,\" a good deed, meets a reward better than the deed. Allah's fadl, His bounty already named, appears in payment. Ten times, or more, or paradise itself, have all been mentioned in the wider sunnah and tafsir as ways this \"better\" is fulfilled. The verse states the principle: good is not paid at a stingy rate.\n\n\"And they will be secure from the horror on that Day.\" The \"faza'\" that seized the heavens and the earth does not own the people of good. Security, \"aminun,\" is safety. Other verses say the greatest terror will not grieve them. This line makes the Trumpet scene livable for the believer.\n\nComing with a good deed means arriving with it as one's provision, a life that produced obedience, faith, and works. It is not a single coin tossed at the last second while the heart stayed a denier, though Allah may forgive as He wills. The chapter's own examples of good are sure faith, prayer, charity, thanks like Solomon's, submission like the queen's, and mindfulness like those delivered from Thamud.\n\nThe verse should not be turned into a claim that one tiny act buys the Day. It should be turned into hope that Allah's payment exceeds the work, and into fear that arriving empty is possible. The next verse will state the opposite arrival: whoever comes with an evil deed is hurled face-first into the Fire, and is only repaid for what he used to do. Better-than-the-deed for good, equal justice for evil, is a pairing that shows both bounty and fairness.",
  "90": "\"And whoever comes with an evil deed will be hurled face-first into the Fire.\" The pair to the previous verse is now spoken. A good deed arrived to a better reward and to safety from horror. An evil deed, \"sayyi'ah,\" arrives to a throw. \"Hurled face-first\" is the English of being overturned on the faces into the Fire. The face, which once wore mockery and pride, meets the ground of punishment first.\n\n\"Are you rewarded except for what you used to do?\" closes the door of complaint. The payment matches the habit, \"ma kuntum ta'maloon,\" what you used to work. No one is burned for a life he did not live. The question form makes the damned admit the justice even if they cannot speak a defense, as an earlier verse left them speechless.\n\nEvil deed here is not only one isolated slip of a believer who still comes with faith. In the Quran's court scenes, coming with the sayyi'ah often means arriving with unbelief and the works that grew from it, the sum of a refused guidance. Believers who sin and repent are treated in other verses with forgiveness. This verse is the dark twin of \"whoever comes with a good deed.\"\n\nThe throwing on the faces answers the chiefs who boasted of strength and the nine who planned by night and the people who expelled the chaste. Their chosen postures of power become a posture of fall.\n\nTogether with verse eighty-nine, the chapter's moral math is complete: better than the good, and not more than the evil. Bounty on one side, pure justice on the other. The remaining verses will send the Prophet back to his mission in Mecca: worship, recite, warn, and praise, while Allah shows signs and is never unaware.",
  "91": "\"Say, O Prophet, 'I have only been commanded to worship the Lord of this city of Mecca.'\" The stories and the Hour now fold back into a personal commission. The Prophet is to declare a limit and a center. He is not commanded to invent a kingdom like Solomon's. He is commanded to worship. The Lord he worships is \"the Lord of this city,\" Mecca, the town of the Kaaba, the town that is persecuting him.\n\n\"Who has made it sacred\" reminds the Quraysh of a gift they love. Mecca is \"harramaha,\" made inviolable, a sanctuary where bloodshed and hunting were restricted, a place of pilgrimage that fed their honor and their trade. That sacredness is not from their idols. It is from the Lord of the city.\n\n\"And to Him belongs everything\" widens the claim from one town to all things. The Lord of Mecca is not a local god. The phrase stops anyone from hearing \"Lord of this city\" as a smaller title.\n\n\"And I am commanded to be one of those who fully submit to Him.\" Again \"muslimin,\" the same word of Solomon's letter and the queen's last sentence. The Prophet of Mecca stands in that line. His religion is submission, not a new tribal cult.\n\nThe verse is an answer to those who said he sought power or foreign tales. His \"only\" is worship and belonging to the submitters. Recitation will be added in the next verse. Together they are the mission. The sacred city that rejects him is still a proof for him, because its sacredness came from the God he calls them to. To love Mecca's sanctuary and deny its Lord is a contradiction the sentence exposes.",
  "92": "\"And to recite the Quran.\" The command that began with worship continues with recitation. The Prophet's public work is to recite this Book that the chapter called clear, a guide, a mercy, a clarifier of differences. He is not told to force results. He is told to make the words heard.\n\n\"Then whoever chooses to be guided, it is only for their own good.\" Guidance chosen is a benefit that returns to the guided, as Solomon said gratitude is for the grateful person's own good. Allah does not need their guided state. They need it.\n\n\"But whoever chooses to stray, say, O Prophet, 'I am only a warner.'\" Straying is also a choice in this wording. The Prophet's response to that choice is a title: \"I am only a warner.\" He is not a controller of hearts, as the verses about the dead, the deaf, and the blind already said. A warner delivers news of danger and of promise. He does not own the road after that.\n\nThe pairing of chosen guidance and chosen straying respects human response without making man the creator of guidance in Allah's independent sense. Other verses say Allah guides whom He wills. This verse speaks from the side of human choosing and responsibility. Both sides are Quranic. Here the stress is that the Prophet's back is not broken by their choice.\n\nThe chapter began as a guide and good news for believers. It ends with the reciter who cannot make everyone into a believer. That honesty is part of why the Book is trusted. It does not pretend a messenger is a magician of souls. The last verse will put praise on his tongue and promise that signs will be shown and recognized, while the Lord remains aware of what they do.",
  "93": "\"And say, 'All praise is for Allah!'\" The chapter that told the Prophet to say praise after the stories of the chosen servants now ends on praise again. \"Al-hamd\" is the right close after commands to worship, recite, and warn. The messenger's last public word in this surah is not a curse. It is praise.\n\n\"He will show you His signs, and you will recognize them.\" Signs already filled the chapter: a staff, a shining hand, an ant's speech, a throne in a blink, crystal like water, ruined homes, a rain of brimstone, night and day, traveling mountains. More signs will be shown in the future of the hearers, in the world and in their own selves, until recognition is unavoidable. \"Fa-ta'rifunaha,\" you will know them as what they are.\n\n\"And your Lord is never unaware of what you do.\" The last clause answers every secret plot, every quiet mockery, every hidden doubt, and every unseen good. Unawareness is denied. The Record mentioned earlier, the knowledge of what chests conceal, the All-Aware who sees deeds while mountains move—all of that is gathered into one simple close.\n\nThe queen recognized a disguised throne. This verse says people will recognize Allah's signs. Recognition can come in time for submission, as it did for her, or too late, as it did for those left speechless. The promise that signs will be shown is therefore both mercy and warning.\n\nSo the chapter of the ant ends where a clear Book should end: praise, coming signs, and a Lord who does not sleep on human action. The next chapter will open with similar letters and another telling of Moses, as if the reciter is immediately given more of the story he was commanded to recite."
};
